    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542824/national-policy-manager": {
      "title": "National Policy Manager",
      "description": "National Policy Manager Apply now Job no: 542824 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management, Governance, Policy & Compliance, Indigenous Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term until 31 December 2027 Remuneration: Level 9 $153,933 - $163,338 + 17% super + Leave Loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Culturally Nourishing Schooling (CNS) project is a practice- and research-informed program aimed at better engaging and supporting the education of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students. Working across universities, local communities and schools, CNS supports culturally focused, strengths-based whole-of-school change and long-term relationships between schools and their local communities. CNS is now in Phase Two (2025-2027), expanding across New South Wales, Victoria and South Australia and working with researchers, communities and stakeholders including government, philanthropic, Aboriginal education consultative groups and university sponsors. As National Policy Manager, you will plan, develop and implement a national policy and advocacy strategy to amplify CNS’s advocacy efforts. Working closely with national and interstate project teams, research schools, stakeholders and funders, you will leverage partner networks to build political commitment and support systems change in the education of First Nations learners and their peers. You will also provide strategic policy advice to the National Chief Investigator and work closely with the National Research Manager and National Communications Manager to translate research findings for diverse audiences, including policy reports and briefing papers. The role reports to the National Chief Investigator, Professor Kevin Lowe, and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Plan, develop and deliver the CNS policy and advocacy strategy, including policy research and analysis, review of key policy initiatives and development of advocacy messaging. Work with the National CNS team and peak bodies to maximise the impact of policy activity at national and state levels. Work collaboratively with schools, Aboriginal staff and community members to support long-term relationships between schools and Aboriginal communities. Build and maintain key government and stakeholder relationships at state and federal levels. Work with project teams and stakeholders to support CNS objectives and advocacy activity. Partner with relevant stakeholders to communicate program outcomes and impact. Work with the National Communications Manager and researchers to translate research findings into accessible policy reports, briefing papers and other communications. Work independently and collaboratively across national and interstate teams. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: A relevant tertiary qualification with proven experience, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience working with diverse groups, including Aboriginal families, communities and peak organisations. Proven experience developing and implementing successful education strategies and plans. Strong advocacy capability, including government relations and/or senior-level political or industry engagement experience. Excellent written communication and presentation skills, including the ability to translate research into accessible content for diverse audiences. Proven ability to work independently, exercise judgement and proactively resolve complex problems, including negotiating outcomes at senior levels. Excellent networking, interpersonal, stakeholder management and negotiation skills, with the ability to influence senior management. Excellent time and workload management skills, with the ability to manage changing priorities, multiple tasks and competing deadlines. An understanding of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander values, perspectives, connections and knowledges that will contribute to policy and advocacy strategies. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV and a 1-2 page cover letter addressing your suitability for the role in relation to the selection criteria (listed under ‘Skills & Experience’ in the Position Description) via the application portal. Applications close: 11:30pm on the 18 th of October A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543052/student-success-participation-officer-indigenous-identified-liverpool-study-hub": {
      "title": "Student Success & Participation Officer (Indigenous Identified) Liverpool Study Hub",
      "description": "Student Success & Participation Officer (Indigenous Identified) Liverpool Study Hub Apply now Job no: 543052 Work type: Full Time Location: Other location Categories: Student Services, Student Recruitment / Admissions, Indigenous Employment Type: Full-time contract role until November 2027 (potential for extension) Remuneration: $108k+ 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Liverpool (On-site role) Benefits: Cultural Leave and Indigenous Employee Network This position is open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of a representative workforce rate. The Liverpool Study Hub is a community-driven space designed to improve tertiary access and boost success for current and future students of any Australian university, TAFE or tertiary provider. Supported by the Australian Government through the Suburban University Study Hubs program , the Hub provides welcoming study spaces, as well as in-person academic, wellbeing, and careers services for students who face increased barriers to study. UNSW is one of the world’s leading universities, committed to a mission to advance Progress for All. Why your Role Matters: As the Student Success & Participation Officer, you will play a key role in strengthening engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and other underrepresented groups to support student participation, retention and success in higher education. Working collaboratively with Hub staff, other educational providers, Gandangara Local Aboriginal Land Council and community organisations, the role builds strong partnerships to bridge gaps in higher education access, connecting prospective and current students with opportunities and support Your day-to-day focus will include: Driving initiatives that improve access, participation and success for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islanders and other underrepresented students in higher education. Delivering culturally responsive support and programs that support current and perspective students’ retention and success. Bringing existing knowledge of higher education pathways to cconnecting students with culturally responsive information, opportunities and referral pathways. Building meaningful community partnerships across schools, education providers and community organisations to champion widening participation and student success Promoting the Liverpool Study Hub through engaging outreach, communications and community engagement activities. Creating a sense of belonging through a culturally safe and inclusive student experience. Who You Are: This position is open to Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander applicants only. UNSW has obtained an exemption under section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) to designate and recruit professional and academic positions for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander persons only, to fulfil UNSW’s goal of a representative workforce rate. Evidence of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander status will be required https://www.indigenous.unsw.edu.au/evidencing-aboriginality-andor-torres-strait-islander-status Bringing a deep understanding of the higher education sector, student lifecycle and the challenges affecting participation and success for students from Indigenous, equity and underrepresented communities, you’ll be confident in developing and delivering student support, outreach or widening participation initiatives that promote access, inclusion, belonging. Key requirements: Relevant degree in education, communications, community services, youth work, marketing or related discipline. Understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures, perspectives, protocols and lived experience. Knowledge of higher education pathways and equity student experiences. Experience delivering student support, outreach or widening participation initiatives. Excellent interpersonal, communication and relationship management. Ability to provide high-quality advisory support, with a commitment to inclusion and culturally responsive practice. Strong organisational, project coordination and digital skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and evaluate activities in a developing environment. Alongside standard pre-employment checks, this role requires the successful candidate to hold a valid paid Working with Children’s Check. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) Establishing your status as an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person means any person who: is of Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent; identifies as an Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person; and is accepted as such by the community in which you live and or have lived. All offers of employment will be subject to prior evidencing of Aboriginal/Torres Strait Islander status. For more information on what constitutes as evidencing Aboriginal/Torres Strait Islander status, please email n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: Nat Chenhall – Manager Indigenous Employment n.chenhall@unsw.edu.au How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter addressing the key selection criteria (1 page) before 11.55pm Sunday 25 October 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application . Job queries contact: Katherine Molloy, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543260/cyber-security-assurance-lead": {
      "title": "Cyber Security Assurance Lead",
      "description": "Cyber Security Assurance Lead Apply now Job no: 543260 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Cyber Full-time continuing role within UNSW IT, Cyber Directorate – Cyber Security Assurance Lead Starting Salary $153,933 plus generous superannuation and leave loading Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Cyber Security Assurance Lead - leads the operational delivery and reporting of the Cyber Security Assurance Services within a fit-for-purpose and adaptive Cyber Security Risk Management framework. Responsible for the controls assurance testing, metrics reporting, and the provision of cyber security subject matter expertise. The Cyber Security Assurance Lead reports to the Cyber Security Controls Assurance Manager and has no direct reports. Accountabilities: Conduct regular review of cyber security controls across IT systems, applications, and networks and perform testing to validate the design and operating effectiveness of controls, identify control gaps, vulnerabilities, and risks, and provide actionable insights and recommendations for remediation. Collaborate with stakeholders to develop remediation plans, track progress toward remediation, and work with business owners, control owners and operators to resolve and close findings/issues. Update the cyber risk register with risks from assurance reviews. Prepare detailed audit reports, including findings, risk impacts, and recommendations for senior management and stakeholders, and communicate and report on control testing outcomes and findings. Develop and maintain dashboards and metrics to monitor the implementation and effectiveness of security controls, and implement, operationally manage and continually improve the quarterly metrics program. Report monthly to the CISO and Deputy CISO on the progress of cyber security assurance. Demonstrate leadership in managing and improving UNSW’s assurance capabilities, including improvements to control assurance methodologies, testing procedures, and reporting processes, (incl. Automation). Support the independent audit of cyber security controls on behalf of the University, including statutory audits completed by the Audit Office of NSW. Continually stay up to date and aware of legal, regulatory compliance and contractual obligations that are relevant to the University’s management of cyber security risk. Undertake other duties appropriately and in line with this position as requested by the Cyber Security Controls Assurance Manager and the Head of Cyber Security Governance & Assurance. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. Ensure hazards and risks of psychosocial and physical are identified and controlled for tasks, projects, and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. Who you are: *5+ years of IT security experience, ideally with global consulting firms, large technology companies, government agencies, or defence consultancies. *Thorough understanding of penetration testing, vulnerability management, application security testing, and source code review. *Experience in a first-line assurance role, assessing controls for design and operating effectiveness; experience in security controls testing is advantageous but not mandatory. *Strong analytical skills with the ability to interpret data and provide actionable insights for management and problem-solving skills to address challenges effectively. *Strong fundamentals in Cyber Security Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC) principles and practices. *Comprehensive knowledge of industry standards and frameworks, such as ISO 27001, NIST 800 53, CSA, Essential 8, PCI DSS, COBIT 5, and MITRE ATT&CK. *Bachelor’s degree in Cyber Security, Information Technology, or a related field. *Relevant industry certifications such as CISA, CISSP, CRISC, or ISO 27001 Lead Auditor (highly desirable). *Strong interpersonal, communication, and negotiation skills, with the ability to build effective relationships and influence stakeholders at all organizational levels. *Capability to present complex technical information in a concise, clear, and credible manner for diverse audiences. *Demonstrated high level of personal motivation, resilience, and ability to work effectively individually or in teams. *An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. *Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits To apply: Please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses addressing the required Who you are criteria. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application . Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Jen MacLachlan - Talent Acquisition Partner- e: j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Please apply through the application portal as we do not accept direct applications to the email above. Applications close : Thursday 22 nd of October at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543395/administrative-assistant": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant Apply now Job no: 543395 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Ranked among the top 50 universities globally, we attract the highest potential students from Australia and around the world. Our researchers tackle the biggest economic, business, and social challenges with partners around the world. Position Summary An Administrative Assistant is responsible for providing, practical and efficient administrative support. This role will: Perform routine administrative tasks such as typing, data entry, emails, diary management, filing, photocopying, accepting/arranging deliveries and couriers, room bookings, catering, mail sorting, ordering and stocking stationery and equipment. Maintain filing systems, spreadsheets, databases and websites. Respond to standard enquiries on UNSW administrative practices. Draft agendas, minutes, correspondence, briefing notes, and guidelines for specific committees and meetings. Induct new staff and visitors including arranging access to systems, buildings and site orientation. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. A person engaged is generally expected to have the following skills and experience: Prior administrative work experience. Attention to detail and organisation skills. Proficient in Microsoft Office software and other online systems. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. If you require any further information regarding the role, please contact Febrina Salihin via f.salihin@unsw.edu.au To submit an application for the role, please do so via the 'Apply Now' button. Please note, no applications will be accepted via email. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Mar 2027 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543611/research-associate-physics": {
      "title": "Research Associate - Physics",
      "description": "Research Associate - Physics Apply now Job no: 543611 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Physics is one of the leading Physics schools in Australia. It offers a world class undergraduate physics degree, as well as a postgraduate PhD research program for over 50 students. 33 academic staff conduct theoretical and experimental research in quantum physics, astrophysics, fundamental physics; and condensed matter physics. UNSW is an international leader in quantum computing research and hosts the ARC Centre for Quantum Computation and Communication Technology. This position supports an ARC Discovery Project, working in collaboration with groups at the University of Melbourne, to develop strategies for the integration of plasmonic nano-tweezers with quantum sensing using near-surface nitrogen-vacancy defect centres. The project is largely experimental in nature and will include elements of nanofabrication, optical instrumentation design, and optical modelling. THIS ROLE IS LOCATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. About the role $118K - $126K plus 17% superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 18 months Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Contribute independently or as a team member in collaborative research with a focus to enhance the quality of research outcomes in the discipline area. Conduct research (as per the norms of the discipline) and/or enable research teams to create scholarly output that is recognised by peers. Undertake specific research project/s under the guidance of a research leader and contribute to development of research activities. Support the dissemination of research outcomes through appropriate channels and outlets. Undertake discipline-appropriate research activities, e.g. surveys, literature reviews, data gathering and/or recording of results using appropriate research methods. Participate in and/or present at conferences and/or workshops relevant to the project as required. Assist with the supervision of research students in the research area where required. Develop experimental instrumentation for incorporating nanoscale optical trapping with diamond-based quantum sensing. Use cleanroom techniques to design and fabricate nano-structured photonic devices suitable for near-field trapping. Perform assays to quantify the magnetic response of optically trapped objects, including single proteins. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Experience in the use of nanofabrications techniques for creating nanophotonic structures and devices. Experience with building custom optical instrumentation with computer interfacing for microscopy applications. Experience conducting biological assays is desirable. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Peter Reece E: p.reece@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 26 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW aspires to be the exemplar Australian university and employer of choice for people from diverse backgrounds. UNSW aims to ensure equality in recruitment, development, retention and promotion of staff and that no-one is disadvantaged on the basis of their gender, cultural background, disability, sexual orientation or identity or Indigenous heritage. We encourage everyone who meets the selection criteria to apply. UNSW partners with Australia’s leading diversity organisations, networks, and campaigns. Please refer to UNSW’s diversity offerings for further information on our flexible work and leave options, and support for carers (childcare, parent rooms, parental leave). Position Description Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543635/research-associate": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate Apply now Job no: 543635 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Psychology at UNSW is the leading Psychology school in Australia. It is located in the Faculty of Science and is recognised nationally and internationally for its excellence in research and teaching.  It offers world class undergraduate psychology degrees, as well as postgraduate professional Masters training in Clinical and Forensic Psychology, an online Graduate Diploma program, and a PhD research program with over 120 students. 40 academic staff conduct experimental research in many areas of Psychology, including behavioural and cognitive neuroscience, perception, learning, memory and cognition, as well as health, social, developmental, clinical and forensic psychology. UNSW Psychology is the only School in the southern hemisphere ranked in the top 30 by discipline in the THES, QS and ARWU rankings. The School has well equipped research and teaching facilities and attracts outstanding staff and students from Australia and overseas. UNSW is located 5 km from the centre of Sydney and is close to other research centres, teaching hospitals, transport, shopping, and beaches. For further information about the school please visit www.psychology.unsw.edu.au This position is specifically required to contribute towards a research program leveraging single-cell transcriptomics (snRNAseq) of brain tissue to understand differences in animal behaviour. The successful candidate will conduct independent research and work closely with collaborators. They will also be responsible for contributing to open-source analysis pipelines, and data governance. About the role LEVEL A - $118k - $126k plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2 years Full time (35 hours) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Contribute independently and collaboratively to a research project requiring collection, processing, analysis, and interpretation of snRNAseq data. Conduct research (as per the norms of the discipline) and/or enable research teams to create high quality scholarly outputs recognised by peers. Undertake specific research project/s under the guidance of a research leader and contribute to development of research activities. Support the dissemination of research outcomes through appropriate channels and outlets. Undertake discipline-appropriate research activities, e.g. literature reviews, data gathering and/or recording of results using appropriate research methods. Participate in and/or present at conferences and/or workshops relevant to the project as required. Assist with the supervision of research students in the research area where required. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) A PhD in a relevant discipline and/or relevant work experience. Demonstrated experience collecting, processing, and analysing gene expression data. Strong quantitative and programming skills and experience developing reproducible analysis pipelines and/or open-source tools for large datasets. Experience with behavioural testing in mice and brain tissue collection and processing (e.g. nuclei isolation and fixation) is preferred. Experience working with animal research ethics and a clear understanding of regulatory and data-governance requirements in neuroscience research. Demonstrated ability to undertake high quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. Evidence of engagement with open-science practices, including code and/or data sharing and the use of open, reproducible workflows Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Philip Jean-Richard-dit-Bressel E: p.jean-richardditbressel@unsw.edu.au Applications close: November 9 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 09 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543658/postdoctoral-fellow": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow Apply now Job no: 543658 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Materials Science and Engineering is one of the top ranked Materials programs in the Asia- Pacific regions and strongly positioned in every facet of materials science and engineering. It offers undergraduate programs in Materials Science and Engineering (Honours), as well as a number of combined degree programs with Chemical Engineering, Biomedical Engineering and Commerce. The Postdoctoral Fellow (Level A) is expected to contribute to research on superconducting materials using a Molecular Beam Epitaxy and Scanning Tunnelling Microscopy (MBE-STM) system and other device fabrication instruments. The aim of the project is to develop superconducting quantum sensors based on emerging quantum materials. About the role $118K - $126K plus 17% superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2 years Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Contribute independently or as a team member in collaborative research with a focus to enhance the quality of research outcomes in the discipline area. Conduct research (as per the norms of the discipline) and/or enable research teams to create scholarly output that is recognised by peers. Undertake specific research project/s under the guidance of a research leader and contribute to development of research activities. Support the dissemination of research outcomes through appropriate channels and outlets. Undertake discipline-appropriate research activities, e.g. surveys, literature reviews, data gathering and/or recording of results using appropriate research methods. Participate in and/or present at conferences and/or workshops relevant to the project as required. Assist with the supervision of research students in the research area where required. Provide maintenance of the MBE-STM facility. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (selection criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in Materials Science and Engineering, or a related field. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated expertise in following fields: Cryogenic Measurement & Instrumentation, Quantum & Mesoscopic Devices, Nanofabrication & Device Integration, and Scanning probe techniques: STM, AFM, MFM, etc. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Zhi Li E: zhi.li5@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 26 th , 2026 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- • National and International Criminal history checks • Entitlement to work and ID checks • Working With Children Checks • Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration • Verification of relevant qualifications • Verification of relevant professional membership • Employment history and reference checks • Financial responsibility assessments/checks • Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543700/executive-assistant-to-the-executive-director-marketing-digital-experience": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to the Executive Director, Marketing & Digital Experience",
      "description": "Executive Assistant to the Executive Director, Marketing & Digital Experience Apply now Job no: 543700 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration Employment Type: Full-Time Fixed Term until the end of May 2028 Remuneration: $108,021–$115,233 + 17% superannuation (Level 6) Location: Kensington UNSW Campus (onsite) Why Your Role Matters The Executive Assistant plays a critical role within the Marketing & Digital Experience (M&DX) team, providing high-level executive support to the Executive Director in a busy, fast-paced and complex environment. This is a hands-on role for an experienced Executive Assistant who can bring structure, clarity and momentum to a demanding executive portfolio. You will take ownership of the Executive Director’s calendar and inbox, prioritise competing demands, ensure urgent matters are addressed, and keep the Executive Director prepared and on track. The role requires someone who is proactive, resilient, highly organised and comfortable diplomatically pushing back when priorities need to be clarified. The Executive Assistant reports to the Marketing Executive Officer but primarily supports the M&DX Executive Director, with broader support to the M&DX Leadership Team as required. The role has no direct reports. This role is approved until May 2028 as an employee replacement. Key responsibilities include: Proactively manage the Executive Director’s calendar, inbox, meetings, priorities and follow-up actions. Anticipate information and operational needs and ensure the Executive Director is well prepared for meetings and key commitments. Act as a trusted first point of contact for internal and external stakeholders, resolving matters where appropriate and escalating sensitive, urgent or complex issues. Coordinate confidential meetings, agendas, papers, minutes, action registers, travel and accommodation arrangements. Manage executive correspondence, documents, financial requirements and confidential information. Maintain accurate filing systems, databases, contact lists, group email lists and other administrative systems. Support the M&DX Hub SharePoint site and contribute to projects, events and business improvement activities. Provide administrative and coordination support to other members of the M&DX Leadership Team as required. Who You Are We are looking for an experienced Executive Assistant who is energised by a busy environment, is proactive and is confident managing competing priorities. You will bring: Experience providing Executive Assistant support to senior management in a large and complex organisation Demonstrated experience managing a senior executive’s calendar and inbox, prioritising competing demands and ensuring actions are followed through Experience preparing executives for meetings, coordinating agendas and papers, arranging travel and accommodation, taking minutes and managing confidential information Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of accuracy and attention to detail Strong judgement, discretion and resilience when managing sensitive matters, changing priorities and time-critical requests Excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to communicate confidently with staff at all levels and external stakeholders Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to work autonomously and proactively identify improvements Advanced computer literacy, including experience with Microsoft Office, Microsoft Teams and other relevant administrative, financial and travel systems This role is predominantly onsite at the UNSW Kensington Campus, with the expectation that the successful applicant will generally work onsite four to five days per week. Occasional flexibility to work remotely one day per week may be available, subject to operational requirements. Candidates will need to have ongoing working rights in Australia. This role is not available for sponsorship. Recruitment process: Please submit your resume and a cover letter highlighting your interest for the role and your experience and/or transferable skills in the role. Applications close: 25 October 2026 11.55PM Get in Touch: Anne de Voronine, Talent Acquisition Associate, a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543702/postdoctoral-fellow-physics": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Physics",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Physics Apply now Job no: 543702 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Physics is one of the leading Physics schools in Australia. It offers a world class undergraduate physics degree, as well as a postgraduate PhD research program for over 50 students. 33 academic staff conduct theoretical and experimental research in quantum physics, astrophysics, fundamental physics; and condensed matter physics. UNSW is an international leader in quantum computing research and hosts the ARC Centre for Quantum Computation and Communication Technology. This position is part of a team led by Prof Dennis Stello within the astrophysics group at UNSW-Sydney’s School of Physics. Astrophysics at UNSW currently has 11 permanent academic staff working on Exoplanets, Stellar evolution, Galactic archaeology, Galaxies, and Cosmology. Professor Stello’s team works predominantly on the asteroseismology of solar-like oscillating stars, including applications on planet-hosting stars, stellar clusters, and Galactic archaeology ( https://newt.phys.unsw.edu.au/~dstello/ ). THIS ROLE IS LOCATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. About the role $118K - $126K plus 17% superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2 years (with possible 6 month extension based on performance and funding) Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Perform research on asteroseismology (particularly solar-like oscillations) in relation to data from space missions like Kepler/K2/TESS/Roman/Gaia (particularly Kepler) and ground-based sources (including Galah/APOGEE/LAMOST). Develop and apply software analysis tools for the analysis, and merging, of large multi-dimensional data sets (time series and single epoch). Publish and contribute to the publication of the research in leading scientific journals. Attend and present at national and international conferences as required. Contribute independently and collaborate with members of the research group. Assist with mentoring/supervision of graduate students and Honours students. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in Astrophysics with a focus on stellar astrophysics and asteroseismology. Demonstrated experience in advanced data analysis and stellar modelling in the realm of stellar astrophysics and asteroseismology (preferably related to solar-like oscillations of red giants including red clump stars and in combining data from multiple data sources). Demonstrated ability to conduct independent research and publish in leading international scientific journals. An excellent research track record (relative to opportunity) in the discipline area as evidenced by conference presentations and publications. Demonstrated experience in presenting research outcomes to audiences such as at seminars and international conferences. Demonstrated ability to co-supervise research students (honours, masters, or PhD). Excellent verbal and written communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. Demonstrated ability to work in a research team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Dennis Stello E: d.stello@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 26 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW aspires to be the exemplar Australian university and employer of choice for people from diverse backgrounds. UNSW aims to ensure equality in recruitment, development, retention and promotion of staff and that no-one is disadvantaged on the basis of their gender, cultural background, disability, sexual orientation or identity or Indigenous heritage. We encourage everyone who meets the selection criteria to apply. UNSW partners with Australia’s leading diversity organisations, networks, and campaigns. Please refer to UNSW’s diversity offerings for further information on our flexible work and leave options, and support for carers (childcare, parent rooms, parental leave). Position Description Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543365/technical-operations-officer-workshop-asset-support": {
      "title": "Technical Operations Officer (Workshop & Asset Support)",
      "description": "Technical Operations Officer (Workshop & Asset Support) Apply now Job no: 543365 Work type: Full Time Location: Canberra, ACT Categories: Administration, Estate / Facilities Management, Research Administration support Technical Operations Officer (Workshop & Asset Support), UNSW Canberra Employment type: Full-time Duration: Continuing/permanent Remuneration: Level 5/6. Level 5 from $ 92,265, Level 6 from $ 108,021 + leave loading + 17% super Location : UNSW Canberra, ADFA Campus Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. About UNSW Canberra University of New South Wales (UNSW) in Canberra has multiple locations in the Nation’s Capital. UNSW Canberra distinguishes itself from other institutions by its commitment to being thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all endeavours. This combined approach is integral to the university's impact and contributes to its recognition as Australia’s #1 university, one of the top 20 universities globally, as well as a proud member of Australia's esteemed Group of Eight. At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW Canberra School of Science offers undergraduate and postgraduate research degrees across established and emerging scientific disciplines, aimed at shaping the future of science.  This is strengthened by cutting-edge facilities and two-way interaction with industry partners, government institutions and the global scientific community. Working in a fast-paced and dynamic environment, the Technical Operations Officer provides general support to the School of Science through the maintenance of its assets and fleet of vehicles. In addition, the Technical Operations Officer will support the staff through the delivery of the management of assets, movement of equipment and furniture as well as participation in WH&S inspections and general School maintenance. You will report to the Manager, Mechanical Workshop. Accountabilities: Duties are varied but include overseeing fleet vehicles and workshop assets. General support to the staff. Development and maintenance of our online tracking systems. Taking deliveries. Coordinating contractors. General maintenance. A commitment to health and safety. Who You Are (skills and experience): At Level 5 • Demonstrated ability to work both independently and effectively within and across teams to build relationships with colleagues, and internal and external stakeholders to deliver on outcomes within prescribed deadlines. • Proficiency in a range of computing skills including word processing, spreadsheets, databases, SharePoint, internet and email and online systems. • Must hold a current driving licence valid in the ACT. • Ability to produce accurate, timely and well-presented information that meets requirements. • An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. • Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. At Level 6 (in addition to the above) • Completion of a trade or WH&S certificate OR completion of Year 12 with relevant work experience OR combination of other relevant education/training and experience. • Ability to learn and contribute to the development and implementation of new systems and procedures. • Demonstrated ability to contribute to, initiate and establish new procedures, guidelines and processes both independently and as a part of a team, escalating issues when necessary. • Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to projects and initiatives. • Excellent communication skills including the ability to consult and liaise with colleagues and internal and external stakeholders at varying levels of authority using demonstrated ability to interact effectively and respectfully with people from differing backgrounds on both technical and non-technical levels. • Ability to draft WH&S documentation. for example, but not limited to, Risk Assessments and Safe Work Procedures. Benefits and Culture: UNSW is committed to helping staff balance work-life responsibilities, by providing access to high-quality services, facilities, and flexible work and leave arrangements. Additional three days leave during December festive period Career development opportunities Clothing allowance Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Free gym access UNSW-wide strategy to focus on Healthy Body, Healthy Mind, Healthy Places and Healthy Culture. You can learn more about our Benefits and Culture at Lifestyle & Benefits UNSW Eligibility: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Make each day matter with a meaningful career at UNSW. Submit your application online before Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11:30pm , including your CV and a 1–2-page cover letter addressing the required skills and experience outlined in the position description. In order to view the position description, please ensure that you allow pop-ups for https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au Get in Touch: For role-related queries please contact: Phillip Donohue - Manager, Mechanical Workshop Manager, Mechanical Workshop For recruitment process-related queries please contact: Holly Whale – Talent Acquisition Partner h.whale@unsw.edu.au Applications cannot be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed. Find out more about the lifestyle and benefits when working with UNSW For further information about UNSW Canberra, please visit: UNSW Canberra For further information on living in Canberra, please visit: Living in Canberra UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. #LI-HW1 Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543368/senior-project-officer": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer",
      "description": "Senior Project Officer Apply now Job no: 543368 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Services, Project Management Employment Type: Full time, Fixed Term 12-month opportunity. Remuneration: Level 7 – from $118,483 + 17% superannuation. Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid work profile) About the Role The Senior Project Officer, UNSW Online plays an important role in supporting the delivery, coordination and continuous improvement of UNSW Online programs within the Lifelong Learning unit. By contributing to Lifelong Learning at UNSW, this role enables Progress for All by expanding access to flexible, high-quality education for learners at different stages of their careers. Working closely with the Operations Manager, UNSW Online, this role provides practical and efficient coordination, administration and operational support across program delivery, course administration, stakeholder engagement, reporting, scheduling and educational activity coordination. The role helps ensure UNSW Online programs are delivered smoothly, consistently and in line with university processes, quality expectations and a high-quality student experience. This position is ideal for a highly organised, collaborative and proactive professional who enjoys working in a growing, dynamic environment and contributing to meaningful lifelong learning initiatives. The Senior Project Officer, UNSW Online reports to the Operations Manager, UNSW Online and has no direct reports. About the Role The successful applicant will be required to undertake and have experience in the following areas of expertise: Provide high-level program, course, project and operational support to the Operations Manager and the broader UNSW Online team, with a strong focus on internal award program operations, education support and course administration. Support the end-to-end coordination, product design, uplift and delivery of UNSW Online education initiatives, facilitating academic readiness, scheduling, faculty expertise integration and effective stakeholder engagement across internal development and design teams. Oversee and deliver assigned Lifelong Learning projects and initiatives, taking responsibility for planning, milestone tracking, resource monitoring, capacity planning and proactive risk and dependency management. Maintain integrated monitoring and reporting across project deliverables, risks, issues and costs, escalating complex matters requiring academic, policy or executive determination in a timely manner. Drive continuous process improvement across UNSW Online and Lifelong Learning by reviewing operational practices, improving information flows, and developing streamlined tools, templates and ways of working. Manage clear, accurate and effective stakeholder communications across internal teams, faculties, academic staff and external partners, while delivering comprehensive administrative support including meeting coordination, minute-taking, briefing materials and action tracking. Ensure strict compliance with UNSW governance pathways, university policies, enterprise agreements and health and safety requirements, while actively modelling UNSW values, mitigating risk and fostering a collaborative, improvement-focused team culture. About You Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience, or equivalent competence gained through a combination of education, training and experience. Proven experience delivering operational, administrative and project coordination within a complex environment, preferably across higher education, online learning or program delivery. Strong project support capabilities, with a sound understanding of the project life cycle, action tracking, milestone reporting, scheduling and developing clear project plans. Highly developed organisational and time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to exercise judgement and initiative when managing competing demands and shifting priorities. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with exceptional attention to detail in producing high-quality documentation, reports, correspondence and project deliverables. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively, build productive relationships with stakeholders at all levels, and operate effectively both independently and as a service-focused team member. Proficiency with the Microsoft Office suite, project management software and enterprise systems, alongside the capacity to quickly adapt to new university platforms and tools. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before Sunday 25 th October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543369/senior-project-officer-shared-services": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer – Shared Services",
      "description": "Senior Project Officer – Shared Services Apply now Job no: 543369 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Services, Project Management Employment Type: Full time, Fixed Term 12-month opportunity. Remuneration: Level 7 – from $118,483 + 17% superannuation. Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid work profile) About the Role The Senior Project Officer, Shared Services, Operations plays an important role in driving operational efficiencies and the quality processing of key administrative tasks, particularly across specialised projects within the Lifelong Learning unit. By contributing to Lifelong Learning at UNSW, this role enables Progress for All by expanding access to flexible, high-quality education for learners at different stages of their careers. Working closely with the Operations Managers across UNSW Online and Short Courses, this position provides targeted administrative coordination and support for broader workforce operations and key operational projects. A primary focus of the role involves overseeing external workforce engagement, including the management of Statements of Work (SOWs), statements of duties, and third-party vendor and casual staffing processes. Additionally, the role supports continuous improvement initiatives across knowledge management, automation, and process optimisation to ensure Lifelong Learning programs run smoothly, efficiently, and in line with university protocols and quality standards. This position is ideal for a highly organised, collaborative and proactive professional who enjoys working in a dynamic environment and driving operational excellence across diverse educational portfolios. The Senior Project Officer, Shared Services, Operations reports to the Head, Operations, Lifelong Learning, and has no direct reports. About the Role The successful applicant will be required to undertake and have experience in the following areas of expertise: Provide high-level project, operational and administrative support to Operations Managers across UNSW Online and Short Courses, supporting portfolio, workforce and financial planning activities. Coordinate external workforce engagements, vendor arrangements and third-party operational requirements, including the administration of Statements of Work (SOWs), statements of duties and casual staffing processes across Lifelong Learning. Support annual and ongoing planning processes by collating inputs for budgets, forecasts, resource capacity, operational priorities and key milestones. Maintain and continuously improve operational systems, tools, registers, knowledge management resources and process automation to enhance service delivery and workflow efficiency. Monitor project and portfolio deliverables, tracking actions, risks, issues and dependencies while ensuring strict adherence to UNSW governance, policy pathways and compliance standards. Facilitate clear, timely stakeholder communications and comprehensive administrative support across internal teams, faculties, academic staff and external partners, including meeting management, minute-taking and briefing material preparation. Promote a collaborative, service-focused team culture that upholds UNSW values, complies with health and safety procedures, and drives practical process improvements across shared services. About You Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience, or equivalent competence gained through a combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience providing operational, administrative and project coordination within a complex environment, preferably across higher education, online learning or shared services. Experience managing operational administrative workflows, with exposure to external workforce coordination, vendor arrangements, contractor engagements or casual staffing processes. Sound understanding of project management principles, including project lifecycle management, action tracking, milestone reporting, scheduling and developing project plans. Strong organisational and time management skills, with a proven ability to exercise judgement and initiative when managing competing demands and shifting priorities. High-level written and verbal communication skills, with exceptional attention to detail in preparing clear documentation, reports, correspondence and briefing materials. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with diverse stakeholders at all levels, operating effectively as a service-focused team member while working independently, alongside proficiency in Microsoft Office, project management tools and enterprise systems. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before Sunday 25 th October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543407/data-analyst-unsw-business-school": {
      "title": "Data Analyst- UNSW Business School",
      "description": "Data Analyst- UNSW Business School Apply now Job no: 543407 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Analyst Employment Type: Full Time, 35 hours per week Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Professional Level 6 from $108,021 plus 17% super Location: Kensington, New South Wales Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Data Analyst supports the UNSW Business School’s accreditation, continuous improvement and strategic decision-making activities through the delivery of high-quality data analysis, insights and reporting. Working closely with stakeholders across the Faculty, the role collects, integrates, analyses and interprets complex data from multiple sources to produce meaningful reports, interactive dashboards and visualisations that support accreditation requirements, curriculum and educational quality initiatives, and evidence-based improvement of student learning and program outcomes including AACSB, EQUIS and PRME. The position manages data capture and reporting processes, supports the development of accessible and reliable data systems, and provides analysis and advice to senior Faculty stakeholders and the Accreditation team. Using tools such as Excel, Power BI, Tableau and programming languages including Python, SQL or R, the Data Analyst transforms large and complex datasets into clear, actionable insights while identifying opportunities to improve data collection, reporting and analytical capability across the Business School. The role requires strong technical and analytical capability, attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities and reporting deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Strong stakeholder management and communication skills are essential, with the ability to translate complex data into clear and meaningful information for a range of audiences. The role reports to the Senior Business Performance & Planning Analyst and has no direct reports. Some Key Skills Required: A relevant tertiary degree with experience in data analysis and data modelling or proven equivalent competency gained through any other combination of education, training, or experience. Demonstrated experience working with large data sets, from a range of structured and unstructured sources to draw insights and produce visualisations. A high competency manipulating and analysing data in Excel and the ability to model and analyse in one or more programming languages (preferably Python, SQL, R). A high competency in data visualisation tools such as Power BI and knowledge of MS Azure’s cloud platform and applications such as Azure Data Factory, Data Bricks & Analysis Services. Demonstrated technical capability to manipulate, analyse and present data in dynamic and visually interesting formats. Experience with international business school accreditation bodies and their standards and requirements, as well as of the Australian higher education sector desirable. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Submit your application including your resume & selection criteria online before Sunday 1 st November at 11:55pm. To be considered for this role, you must address the Selection Criteria which are outlined in the “Skills and Experience” section of the position description. Applications that do not address these criteria will not be considered Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: - National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543513/technical-officer": {
      "title": "Technical Officer",
      "description": "Technical Officer Apply now Job no: 543513 Work type: Part Time Location: Port Macquarie, NSW Categories: Research Infrastructure Technical Officer, Rural Clinical Campuses Employment Type: Part - time (21 hours a week) Duration: Fixed Term contract until 30 th June 2028 Remuneration : $92,265 base pro rata, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. WWCC: A current Working With Children Check (WWCC) is a requirement of the role. Location: Port Macquarie, NSW UNSW Rural (Port Macquarie campus) UNSW Rural brings world-class medical education to rural and regional Australia, delivering the full 6-year medical course at our rural campuses across several locations. Delivery of the medicine program across UNSW Rural Clinical Campuses is primarily face-to-face - on campus, with staff being available and accessible daily. Students build strong relationships and become part of the local medical community, benefitting from more personal interaction with clinicians, other academics and professional staff. UNSW’s program has strongly supported the aims of the Australian Government Department of Health and Aged Care , Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training (RHMT) program delivering excellence in rural medical education and student experience end-to-end. Local students – Local doctors - the next generation rural medical workforce. Details of the role The Technical Officer works closely with academic staff, laboratory demonstrators and students to provide high level quality technical support in teaching and research laboratories with a strong focus on WHS compliance and requirements. This is an excellent opportunity for a technically skilled professional to contribute to the delivery of high-quality rural teaching and research activities in a dynamic laboratory environment. You will play a key role in ensuring laboratories operate efficiently and safely while supporting practical learning and research outcomes. The position offers varied responsibilities, including equipment and compliance management, laboratory preparation, technical training, troubleshooting and inventory responsibility. Ideal candidates will bring strong biomedical sciences knowledge, excellent organisational skills and a proactive approach to problem-solving. This role is well suited to someone who enjoys working collaboratively, providing expert technical support and helping create an outstanding laboratory experience for students, and supporting staff to deliver practical components of the course. The role of Technical Officer reports to the Administrative Officer at the identified campus and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: A relevant qualification or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Demonstrated knowledge and experience in Biomedical Sciences with extensive understanding of the relevant discipline and/or similar level of experience working in a teaching laboratory. Ensure efficient running of teaching and/or research laboratories through the provision of quality technical support including preparation setup and prior testing of practical components to ensure satisfactory class delivery and turnaround of practical classes and/or labs. Provide technical assistance to students and staff, including demonstration of common techniques and the operation of equipment and adapt practicals by identifying suitable technical alternatives, where required. Provide induction, instrument training and troubleshooting for staff and students ensuring expert support during the delivery of classes. Demonstrated ability to maintain effective operations of specialist laboratories to a high standard. Demonstrated capacity to write safe working procedures and manage risks compliant within UNSW guidelines. Ability to find technical solutions to teaching and research needs. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Sunday 25th  October before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543514/technical-officer": {
      "title": "Technical Officer",
      "description": "Technical Officer Apply now Job no: 543514 Work type: Part Time Location: Wagga Wagga, NSW Categories: Research Infrastructure Technical Officer, Rural Clinical Campuses Employment Type: Part - time (21 hours a week) Duration: Fixed Term contract until 30 th June 2028 Remuneration : $92,265 base pro rata, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. WWCC: A current Working With Children Check (WWCC) is a requirement of the role. Location: Wagga Wagga, NSW UNSW Rural (Wagga Wagga campus) UNSW Rural brings world-class medical education to rural and regional Australia, delivering the full 6-year medical course at our rural campuses across several locations. Delivery of the medicine program across UNSW Rural Clinical Campuses is primarily face-to-face - on campus, with staff being available and accessible daily. Students build strong relationships and become part of the local medical community, benefitting from more personal interaction with clinicians, other academics and professional staff. UNSW’s program has strongly supported the aims of the Australian Government Department of Health and Aged Care , Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training (RHMT) program delivering excellence in rural medical education and student experience end-to-end. Local students – Local doctors - the next generation rural medical workforce. Details of the role The Technical Officer works closely with academic staff, laboratory demonstrators and students to provide high level quality technical support in teaching and research laboratories with a strong focus on WHS compliance and requirements. This is an excellent opportunity for a technically skilled professional to contribute to the delivery of high-quality rural teaching and research activities in a dynamic laboratory environment. You will play a key role in ensuring laboratories operate efficiently and safely while supporting practical learning and research outcomes. The position offers varied responsibilities, including equipment and compliance management, laboratory preparation, technical training, troubleshooting and inventory responsibility. Ideal candidates will bring strong biomedical sciences knowledge, excellent organisational skills and a proactive approach to problem-solving. This role is well suited to someone who enjoys working collaboratively, providing expert technical support and helping create an outstanding laboratory experience for students, and supporting staff to deliver practical components of the course. The role of Technical Officer reports to the Administrative Officer at the identified campus and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: A relevant qualification or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Demonstrated knowledge and experience in Biomedical Sciences with extensive understanding of the relevant discipline and/or similar level of experience working in a teaching laboratory. Ensure efficient running of teaching and/or research laboratories through the provision of quality technical support including preparation setup and prior testing of practical components to ensure satisfactory class delivery and turnaround of practical classes and/or labs. Provide technical assistance to students and staff, including demonstration of common techniques and the operation of equipment and adapt practicals by identifying suitable technical alternatives, where required. Provide induction, instrument training and troubleshooting for staff and students ensuring expert support during the delivery of classes. Demonstrated ability to maintain effective operations of specialist laboratories to a high standard. Demonstrated capacity to write safe working procedures and manage risks compliant within UNSW guidelines. Ability to find technical solutions to teaching and research needs. Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Sunday 25th  October before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543554/research-ethics-compliance-officer": {
      "title": "Research Ethics & Compliance Officer",
      "description": "Research Ethics & Compliance Officer Apply now Job no: 543554 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Administration support, Governance, Policy & Compliance, Clinical/Medical Research Employment Type: Full time (35 hours week), Continuing (permanent) position Salary: $118,483 - $128,233 + 17% super Location: Kensington, NSW Why your role matters Work on challenging and varied issues that combine ethics, legislation, governance and practical problem solving. Flexible/hybrid working arrangements that balance in-person engagement with working from home. Collaborative team environment. Research Ethics & Compliance Officer supports UNSW’s research ethics and compliance activities, with a particular focus on animal research ethics. The role coordinates and supports ethics committees, provides specialist advice to researchers and other stakeholders, and helps ensure research complies with relevant legislation, policies and regulatory requirements. A key part of the role is improving animal ethics processes, guidance and training, including online resources and face-to-face training. You’ll also contribute to the development of policies, procedures and strategic and operational plans aimed at improving the quality of animal research and animal care across UNSW. The role reports to the Manager, Research Ethics & Compliance and has no direct reports. About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Hybrid/flexible working arrangements and are committed to supporting work-life balance while delivering meaningful outcomes. 17% superannuation contributions, annual leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice option Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Affordable on-campus parking About you: You have a relevant tertiary qualification and or experience in animal research, including a strong understanding of the ethical considerations involved in developing and conducting animal research projects. You’ll also be able to demonstrate your comprehensive knowledge of animal research ethics legislation, codes and compliance frameworks, together with strong analytical, critical thinking and creative problem-solving skills to assess ethics applications, interpret complex requirements and develop practical solutions to ethical and compliance issues. Strong written and verbal communication, attention to detail and stakeholder management skills are also essential, as the role provides expert advice and guides researchers through the ethical review process. The role also requires the ability to provide high-quality secretariat support to ethics committees, analyse and apply research policies and guidelines, maintain accurate electronic records, and work in accordance with UNSW policies, values and health and safety requirements. How to apply: Please submit your CV via the Apply button before 11.55pm, Sunday 25 th October 2026. As part of the application process, you will also be asked to respond to three short free-format questions designed to help us better understand your experience.  A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Pre Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Shiree Thomas – Talent Acquisition Partner: shiree.thomas@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contact person will not be accepted NSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-ST1 Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543585/research-assistant": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant Apply now Job no: 543585 Work type: Part Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Clinical/Medical Research Research Assistant Employment Type: Part - time (14 hours a week) Duration: 12 Months Fixed Term contract Remuneration : $92,265 base pro rata, plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Work rights required: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold full rights to work in Australia to be considered for this position. Location: Onsite, Randwick, Sydney About UNSW School of Clinical Medicine : UNSW Medicine & Health is a national leader in learning, teaching and research, with close affiliations to several of Australia’s finest hospitals, research institutes and health care organisations. With a strong presence at UNSW Kensington campus, the faculty have staff and students in teaching hospitals in Sydney as well as regional and rural areas of NSW including Albury/Wodonga, Wagga Wagga, Coffs Harbour and Port Macquarie. Details of the role The Research Assistant provides effective administrative and research assistance to research studies investigating the support needs of children, adolescents, and young adults with chronic health conditions within the Behavioural Sciences Unit/ School of Clinical Medicine, UNSW Medicine & Health. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to meaningful research that aims to improve the lives of children, adolescents and young adults living with chronic health conditions. Working within a collaborative research environment, you will support a range of studies through participant coordination, data management, literature reviews, manuscript preparation and research administration. You will play an important role in supporting innovative projects, including the development of new digital health initiatives and multi-site research programs. The position is well suited to a motivated and organised individual who is passionate about health research, enjoys working with diverse stakeholders, and can effectively balance multiple priorities while maintaining a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. The role of Research Assistant reports to the Associate Professor and has no direct reports. Some key skills required: A relevant tertiary qualification in health, psychology, science or a related discipline or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Previous experience providing research assistance for studies in a health-related field, such as psycho-oncology, or clinical trials research will be highly regarded. Assist with the management of a multi-site initiative supporting young people with chronic illness transition from paediatric to adult care. Coordinate study participation activities via the REDCap survey platform, including survey mail outs and respondent follow ups. Assist with research literature searches for relevant articles and assist with preparation of manuscripts. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail. Previous experience writing for publication will be highly regarded. Knowledge of and aptitude to learn various IT software, such as research-related computer programs (REDCap, SPSS, and Endnote). Please refer to the position description for full details. UNSW Benefits and Culture 17% Superannuation and leave loading​ Flexible working ​ Additional 3 days of leave over the Christmas Period​ Access to lifelong learning and career development ​ Progressive HR practices​ More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. How to apply: Please click on Apply now to apply online. Applications should not be sent to the contact listed below. Please provide a resume and a separate document addressing the skills and experience listed in the Position Description. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW . Contact Frank McHenry, Talent Acquisition Associate E: f.mchenry@unsw.edu.au Applications close: Sunday 25 th October before 11.30pm Sydney time UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543602/project-officersenior-project-officer-arc-coecsi": {
      "title": "Project Officer/Senior Project Officer (ARC COE-CSI)",
      "description": "Project Officer/Senior Project Officer (ARC COE-CSI) Apply now Job no: 543602 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Research Administration support, Marketing / Communications The Opportunity The ARC Centre of Excellence for Carbon Science and Innovation (ARC COE-CSI), led by UNSW, is seeking a Project Officer/Senior Project Officer to provide project, research administration and operational support across the Centre. You will work closely with Centre leadership, researchers and partner universities to support the day-to-day operation of the Centre and help coordinate the delivery of its research, reporting and strategic activities. The role will involve coordinating project activities, reporting processes, workshops, seminars and events, maintaining Centre systems and website content, preparing communications and promotional materials, and providing high-level administrative and secretariat support. You will also contribute to stakeholder engagement, outreach activities and the development of Centre reports and communications. At Senior Project Officer level, you will take on greater responsibility and work independently for KPI monitoring, budgeting and financial analysis, policy development, annual reporting, outreach programs and communication and engagement initiatives. You will bring relevant qualifications and experience in project, program or event coordination, together with strong written communication, organisational and stakeholder engagement skills. You will be confident managing competing priorities, working across a complex multi-stakeholder environment and producing high-quality communications, reports and digital content. At Senior Project Officer level, you will also bring demonstrated experience managing complex programs or events, working independently and contributing to budgeting, reporting and continuous process improvement. This role reports to the Chief Operating Officer and has no direct reports. Salary - Level 6/7: Level 6 - $108,021 to $115,233 per annum + 17% superannuation Level 7 - $118,483 to $128,233 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full time Appointment type: Fixed-term contract – 12-months with possible extension Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The ARC Centre of Excellence for Carbon Science and Innovation (ARC COE-CSI) is a major 7-year initiative supported primarily by the Australian Research Council (ARC). The Centre is led by UNSW and partnering with a group of seven Australian universities and a suite of outstanding national and international Partner Organisations. The Centre’s purpose is to revolutionise carbon science and innovation for renewable energy generation and clean chemical production by using carbon catalysts as alternatives to critical minerals. For more information on the ARC COE-CSI go to https://www.carboncentre.org.au/ Skills and Experience Level 6 Relevant tertiary qualifications with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Advanced communication skills, including demonstrated proficiency in content writing, editing and proofreading, along with the ability to research and analyse material and data to summarise key issues for use in official communications, promotions and reports. Creative content development skills, and experience in designing promotional campaigns and developing and managing digital content for web and social channels, including demonstrated ability to tailor content and messaging to audiences and channels. Demonstrated experience in event/project/program coordination with an understanding of the project life cycle and development of project plans and objectives including managing logistics, promotions, communications, documentation and reporting. Demonstrated high-level interpersonal and communication skills and demonstrated ability to build and maintain positive and professional relationships with staff at all levels and internal and external stakeholders. Excellent organisational and time management skills, accuracy and attention to detail, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, but also to take initiative and work independently while managing competing demands. Advanced level of computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications, web content management, analytics and reporting tools for website engagement and digital communications. Basic graphic design and simple video editing skills, and familiarity with the Adobe Creative Suite or other respective platforms are strongly desirable. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Level 7 (in addition to the above) Proven ability to manage high-value programs and events, from conception to completion, including evaluation and continuous improvement. Excellent writing, analytical, and presentation skills, including the ability to produce high-quality reports and briefing materials. Desired experience in financial administration, including budget monitoring, procurement, purchase orders, invoice processing, reimbursements, credit card reconciliation and financial reporting. Demonstrated experience working independently in complex, multi-stakeholder environments with competing priorities and tight timelines. Additional details about the specific responsibilities of this position can be found in the position description, which is available via JOBS@UNSW. To Apply: Please click the “Apply Now” button and submit your CV, cover letter and responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. To be considered, applicants must have full working rights in Australia and be able to work on-site at our Kensington campus. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: For role-specific inquiries, please contact Jane Su (Chief Operating Officer) E: jane.su@unsw.edu.au For questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact Allyssar Hamoud (Talent Acquisition Associate) E: a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Sunday, 25 October 2026 As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543613/postdoctoral-fellow": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow Apply now Job no: 543613 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Biotechnology & Biomolecular Science (BABS) teaches undergraduates, mentors postgraduate research students and conducts research in the disciplines of biotechnology, biochemistry, genetics, molecular biology, microbiology, environmental microbiology, medical microbiology and immunology. The role is funded by Revive and Restore as a part of the Golden Genome Project ‘Algal Genetic Engineering for Climate Resilience’ https://reviverestore.org/genetic-engineering-for-climate-resilience/. The project aims to leverage deletion library generation approaches to identify climate resilience determining loci in microalgae with small genomes and apply novel CRISPR mediate gene editing to Ecklonia radiata to improve climate resilience in the marine environment. About the role $118K - $126K plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2 years Full time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Contribute independently or as a team member in collaborative research with a focus to enhance the quality of research outcomes in the discipline area. Conduct research (as per the norms of the discipline) and/or enable research teams to create scholarly output that is recognised by peers. Undertake specific research project/s under the guidance of a research leader and contribute to development of research activities. Support the dissemination of research outcomes through appropriate channels and outlets. Undertake discipline-appropriate research activities, e.g. surveys, literature reviews, data gathering and/or recording of results using appropriate research methods. Participate in and/or present at conferences and/or workshops relevant to the project as required. Assist with the supervision of research students in the research area where required. Lead construction of insertional deletion libraries of microalgal species. Assist in development of development of transformation protocols for Ecklonia radiata. Lead maintenance and culture of microalgal strains, and cryopreservation where appropriate. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Experience with microalgal culture and transformation, and expertise in molecular biology. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high-quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Hugh Goold E: h.goold@unsw.edu.au Applications close: November 5 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW aspires to be the exemplar Australian university and employer of choice for people from diverse backgrounds. UNSW aims to ensure equality in recruitment, development, retention and promotion of staff and that no-one is disadvantaged on the basis of their gender, cultural background, disability, sexual orientation or identity or Indigenous heritage. We encourage everyone who meets the selection criteria to apply. UNSW partners with Australia’s leading diversity organisations, networks, and campaigns. Please refer to UNSW’s diversity offerings for further information on our flexible work and leave options, and support for carers (childcare, parent rooms, parental leave). Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 05 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543643/claims-officer": {
      "title": "Claims Officer",
      "description": "Claims Officer Apply now Job no: 543643 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Analyst At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Ranked among the top 50 universities globally, we attract the highest potential students from Australia and around the world. Our researchers tackle the biggest economic, business, and social challenges with partners around the world. Position Summary The Claims Officer plays a key role in a specialised team responsible for identifying, assessing, quantifying and rectifying employee pay claims. The Claims Officer will work closely with the Claims Manager to support the investigation of pay claims, applies relevant industrial instruments and legislation, and contributes to the consistent application of remediation methodologies. The role reports to the Claims Manager within the People & Culture, Quality and Compliance Team. This role will: Review and investigate employee pay claims, including gathering and analysing relevant employment, payroll and supporting records. Apply established claim assessment methodologies, prepare claim recommendations, and assist in determining claim outcomes in accordance with UNSW’s obligations under its enterprise agreements, the Fair Work Act and other applicable regulatory instruments. Liaise with Payroll Compliance, Finance, Salaries and other stakeholders to support claim calculations and remediation activities. Communicate professionally and sensitively with current and former employees throughout the claims process, including managing claimant enquiries and requests for information. Manage administrative duties in relation to reporting on claims received, remediated and trends or broader compliance issues identified. Escalating complex or sensitive matters to the Claims Manager when necessary. A person engaged is generally expected to have the following skills and experience: Relevant degree with experience providing advice on employee relations matters including conducting investigations into pay claims or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated consultation skills and proven ability to engage effectively with diverse stakeholders to achieve successful outcomes. Sound analytical and problem-solving skills and proven capacity to exercise initiative, discretion, flexibility and to be proactive in the development of robust solutions to problems. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Ability to apply judgement, creativity, and flexibility to generate new and innovative ideas and approaches and to solve complex problems. High level of experience working with a range of computer systems and applications, including Microsoft Word and Excel. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. Additional pre-employment checks required for this position Nil Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. If you require any further information regarding the role, please Elin Michaj-Engelin via e.michajengelin@unsw.edu.au To submit an application for the role, please do so via the 'Apply Now' button. Please note, no applications will be accepted via email. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 09 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543424/research-associate-school-of-civil-environmental-engineering-unsw-kensington": {
      "title": "Research Associate – School of Civil & Environmental Engineering (UNSW Kensington)",
      "description": "Research Associate – School of Civil & Environmental Engineering (UNSW Kensington) Apply now Job no: 543424 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate The Opportunity: This project aims to develop an innovative scaled boundary framework leveraging the power of modern computer facilities needed for dynamic nonlinear analysis of large-scale structures. Modern digital technologies for geometric modelling will be seamlessly integrated with computational mechanics. The outcome of this project is an innovative technology and a computer simulation tool that will be robust, fully automated and highly efficient. The role of Research Associate reports to Professor Chongmin Song and has no direct reports. Salary, Level A - $95,036 - $126,711 per annum + 17% superannuation Full Time: 35 hours Fixed-term contract for 12 months Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Faculty of Engineering at UNSW continues to be a national and international leader in engineering research and education, cementing its status as the University’s premier research faculty. As the largest Engineering Faculty in Australia, it graduates the highest number of engineers and maintains the largest research expenditure in the country. Through excellence in both fundamental and applied research, the Faculty strives to sustain and further its global impact, aiming to be ranked among the top engineering faculties worldwide. For more information, visit the Faculty website: https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/about-us Skills & Experience: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Demonstrated ability to undertake high-quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Previous experience on developing numerical methods as evidenced by journal publications. Strong ability and skills in computer programming as demonstrated by PhD research and refereed journal publications. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. • Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for these positions can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW . To Apply: Please click the apply now button and submit your CV, Cover Letter and Responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Please note any applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: Allyssar Hamoud – Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Monday 9 th November 2026 UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 08 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 09 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543356/senior-research-associate-materials-science-and-engineering": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate - Materials Science and Engineering",
      "description": "Senior Research Associate - Materials Science and Engineering Apply now Job no: 543356 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Senior Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Materials Science and Engineering is one of the top ranked Materials programs in the Asia- Pacific regions and strongly positioned in every facet of materials science and engineering. It offers undergraduate programs in Materials Science and Engineering (Honours), as well as a number of combined degree programs with Chemical Engineering, Biomedical Engineering and Commerce. The Senior Research Associate is expected to carry out independent and/or team research under the broad scope of advanced materials and manufacturing, with a specific focus in, but not limited to, physical properties characterisations of functional materials, advanced material fabrications with solid state reaction, vacumm-depsotions, and printing technologies. The position will focus on developing next-generation advanced high-throughput fabrications of multifunctional materials for electronic, energy harvesting, catalytic and many other applications. About the role $133K - $156K plus 17% superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 4 years Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Engage in individual and/or collaborative research in a manner consistent with disciplinary practice. Create scholarly impact in the discipline, which is recognised by peers in the advancement of disciplinary knowledge. Conduct research/scholarly activities under limited supervision, either independently or as a member of a team (as per the norms of the discipline). Establish a personal research portfolio and start developing independent research proposals. Contribute to the development of applications for competitive funding under the guidance of senior colleagues. Participate as co-investigator or chief investigator in competitive grant applications or show evidence of active participation in research collaborations funded by competitive grants. Design research projects. Mentor and guide students and colleagues and develop the next generation of academics through involvement in supervision of HDRs (as per the norms of the discipline). Manage UNSW key laboratory, user training and create an effective system-based schedule to ensure efficient usage for the day-to-day activities of printing equipment. The activities in laboratory management include to create and update safety work procedures, Risk Management Forms and managing relevant safety protocols in the laboratories. Conduct all required monitoring, maintenance, and calibration to ensure functioning of equipment and facilities. Establish partnerships with the leading manufacturing companies. Strengthen collaborations with other research organisations. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the School and University and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (selection criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in Materials Science and Engineering or related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Demonstrated experience in managing and operating key laboratory infrastructures for material research, such as physical and magnetic property measurement systems, thin-film fabrications, solid-state and/or wet-chemical material synthesis facilities, and printing equipment. Demonstrated experience supervising higher-degree-research students at both Master and PhD levels. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated track record in research in the area of functional materials (e.g., thin solid films, electronic materials, energy materials, and catalysts) with outcomes of high quality and high impact with clear evidence of the desire and ability to continually achieve research excellence as well as the capacity for research leadership. High level communication skills and ability to network effectively and interact with a diverse range of students and staff. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Sean Li E: sean.li@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 22 nd , 2026 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- • National and International Criminal history checks • Entitlement to work and ID checks • Working With Children Checks • Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration • Verification of relevant qualifications • Verification of relevant professional membership • Employment history and reference checks • Financial responsibility assessments/checks • Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 08 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543432/senior-learning-designer-lms-transformation": {
      "title": "Senior Learning Designer - LMS Transformation",
      "description": "Senior Learning Designer - LMS Transformation Apply now Job no: 543432 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Student Services, Analyst Employment Type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration : 2 years Fixed-term contract (possibility of 1 year extension) Remuneration: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus – Hybrid work arrangement (2-3 days onsite) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW's LMS transformation is a critical enabler for teaching and learning across the University. As Senior Learning Designer, you will sit at the intersection of academic practice, curriculum renewal and digital learning, helping shape the primary digital interface for UNSW's educational delivery. Working across the Division of Education and Student Experience, Faculties, Schools and central units, you will build empathetic and influential relationships with academic staff and leaders, creating the conditions, frameworks and support that enable high-quality curriculum aligned with UNSW's Progress for All strategy. You will bring expert guidance on curriculum and educational design, Universal Design for Learning, assessment and feedback, digital assessment platforms and emerging pedagogical trends. Your research and advice will help UNSW evaluate innovation, improve practice and lead meaningful change. Working with the LMS Program leadership team and reporting to the Learning Design Director, you will influence university-wide solutions, guide initiatives from concept and pilot through to operation, and lead through collaboration, service orientation and continuous improvement. Responsibilities Summary: Collaborate with University staff to design enterprise LMS solutions, build capability, elevate educational excellence and improve the experience of staff and students. Build strong, influential relationships with academic and professional stakeholders through consultation, advice and change management, supporting innovative teaching strategies and curriculum renewal. Research the latest pedagogical innovations and lead the systematic evaluation and trial of emerging educational design approaches and learning technologies. Manage and transition LMS initiatives in an agile environment from concept and pilot through to fully operational solutions, with agreed deliverables, governance and timeframes. Act as a delegated deputy project lead where required, lead a small team or project workstream, and coordinate delivery across the LMS transformation lifecycle. Contribute to the Innovation Pillar of UNSW's Progress for All Strategy, supporting the development, implementation and operation of services, systems and protocols that advance teaching and learning. Prepare high-quality reports, recommendations and decision material in line with governance protocols for business-as-usual and LMS strategic initiatives. Interpret requirements and provide leadership, strategic advice and solution-focused actions for technology-enabled learning and teaching practices across UNSW. Maintain current and emerging knowledge of digital assessment platforms and pedagogical trends, translating that knowledge into practice improvement and professional development. Provide responsive, student-centred support to students, Faculties, Schools and central units within agreed service expectations, contributing to a culture of professionalism, accountability and continuous service improvement. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualifications in education, learning design or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience supporting academic staff in higher education, preferably in assessment, curriculum design or digital learning contexts. Working knowledge of learning technologies used in assessment, such as Moodle, Turnitin, Inspera or similar platforms. Understanding of contemporary assessment principles and effective feedback practices, including authentic assessment, formative feedback and constructive alignment. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to explain educational concepts clearly and work collaboratively across diverse academic teams. Demonstrated experience designing, developing and facilitating professional development workshops in face-to-face, hybrid and online contexts. Demonstrated organisational and project management skills, with the ability to planning and coordination of multiple work priorities, project and governance reporting requirements, planning and deliver to deadlines. A proactive, student-centred approach to problem-solving, with attention to detail and a commitment to continuous improvement and service excellence. Competency with Microsoft 365 and online platforms and systems, together with a demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion in curriculum and learning design. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW's values, strategic priorities and approach to teaching and learning excellence, together with relevant health and safety responsibilities. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal before 11:30pm on Thursday, the 22 nd of October 2026. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries about the recruitment process, please contact the Talent Acquisition Partner, Ayla Kamal on: ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au For queries about the role, please contact the LMS Program Director, Dean MacDonald on: dean.macdonald@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 08 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543569/postdoctoral-fellow-chemistry": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry Apply now Job no: 543569 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Chemistry teaches more than 2000 undergraduate students each year from many different faculties and offers four different undergraduate programs leading to majors in chemistry that are taught in five teaching laboratories specialising in First Year, Synthetic, Analytical and Physical Chemistry. This role and contributes to the development of novel electrocatalysts and ion exchange membranes for electrolyzers and fuel cells. The Postdoctoral Fellow is also responsible for synthesising, prototyping, and scaled-up demonstration, along with assessing key metrics of efficiency and durability, relating these metrics to product performance and life cycle cost, and developing relevant experiments under schedule constraints. THIS ROLE IS LCOATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. About the role $118K - $126K plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 12 months (extension possible based on performance and or funding) Full-time (35 hours per week) The role reports to Professor Chuan Zhao and Dr Quentin Meyer and has no direct reports. Specific responsibilities for this role include: Contribute independently or as a team member in collaborative research with a focus to enhance the quality of research outcomes in the discipline area. Conduct research (as per the norms of the discipline) and/or enable research teams to create scholarly output that is recognised by peers, including by preparing manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals. Undertake specific research project/s under the guidance of a research leader and contribute to development of research activities. Support the dissemination of research outcomes through appropriate channels and outlets. Undertake other discipline-appropriate research activities, e.g. surveys, literature reviews, data gathering and/or recording of results using appropriate research methods. Prepare and maintain experimental materials, equipment, and data. Prototype electrochemical devices based on research results. Actively apply for external grants and fellowships with support from the supervisor. Assist with student supervision and lab management, including instrument maintenance, troubleshooting, and contacting service providers when required. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in Chemistry, Chemical Engineering or Materials Science. Demonstrated research experience or expertise in the following: Electrocatalysis and/or ion exchange membranes; Material synthesis and device assembly; and Material characterisation. Prior experience in DFT calculations is preferred but not compulsory. Demonstrated experience in conducting a research project (including experiment design, data collection, and record-keeping) and coordinating the efforts of a large multidisciplinary team. Demonstrated ability to complete projects in a timely manner and submit reports. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated ability to undertake high-quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Demonstrated track record of publications in high-impact journals and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Demonstrated experience and ability in student supervision. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and interact with a diverse range of stakeholders and students. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed above in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Chuan Zhao E: chuan.zhao@unsw.edu.au Applications close: November 5 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 08 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 05 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543727/training-manager": {
      "title": "Training Manager",
      "description": "Training Manager Apply now Job no: 543727 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: 10 month day rate contract role as a Training Manager in UNSW IT Location: Kensington campus in Sydney NSW (Hybrid Flexible Working) minimum 2 days in the office. About UNSW: UNSW is a leading global university with a diverse and talented community committed to purposeful, impactful work. Ranked among the world’s top 50 universities, and a member of Australia’s group of Eight, UNSW is known for excellence in education, research and innovation. This Training Manager role supports the embedment of operational change across the UNSW technology delivery portfolio, specifically upgrades to the Admissions systems and processes. They do this by ensuring UNSW staff and students have the ability to use new or improved technology in their day-to-day work. The primary role of the Training Manager is to conduct learning needs analysis and subsequently design, develop, and implement effective and appropriate training activities to maximise adoption of this strategic, operational and technology change initiative. The role reports to the program Change Lead and has no direct reports. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Work closely with the technical SMEs and the Change Lead to understand the technology and the impacts associated with the change. Conduct the learning needs analysis. Develop a fit for purpose learning approach and plan and develop a training strategy through analysis of the impacts of the proposed change. Develop best fit learning resources across multiple available channels and media. Use an effective mix of traditional and digital media, tailoring to impacted stakeholders’ preferences. Produce high-quality learning resources and ensure training activities are delivered within set timeframes. Consult and work collaboratively with key stakeholders and use subject matter expertise to develop and deliver learning experiences. Actively seek feedback to help measure the effectiveness of stakeholder engagement. Align with and actively demonstrate the UNSW Values in Action: Our Behaviours and the UNSW Code of Conduct . Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. Skills and Experience: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent extensive experience or an equivalent level of skills and knowledge gained through any combination of education, training, and experience. Demonstrated experience in developing and delivering training plans to support organisational change. Demonstrated experience in distilling complex information into simple messages with the ability to tailor content for a variety of audiences. Knowledge of both traditional and modern training methods, such as mentoring, coaching, on-the-job or classroom training, e-learning, workshops, scenarios and simulations. Excellent communication and leadership abilities to effectively manage training programs and collaborate with teams. Ability to work in a highly adaptable and consultative manner, working in a collaborative way to deliver the best outcomes. Proficiency in evaluating training program effectiveness and gathering feedback from participants to improve future sessions. Knowledge of change management principles, especially when implementing new training initiatives or processes. Excellent time management and organisational skills with the ability to navigate through competing deadlines. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s goals, objectives, and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Ability to use tools such as Camtasia, SnagIt, Articulate Storyline, Articulate Rise, Adobe Premiere Pro, Adobe Illustrator. Experience with AI tools. To Apply: If this is of interest to you, please submit your CV, Cover Letter and responses to the who you are section. More Information: https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/ Contact Jen MacLachlan, Talent Partner, UNSW IT. j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Applications close:   Sunday 18th of October at 11.30pm UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment Advertised: 08 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543473/postdoctoral-fellow-school-of-photovoltaic-and-renewable-energy-engineering": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral Fellow – School of Photovoltaic and Renewable Energy Engineering",
      "description": "Post-doctoral Fellow – School of Photovoltaic and Renewable Energy Engineering Apply now Job no: 543473 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate The Opportunity: The Post-Doctoral Fellow (Level A) is expected to contribute towards the research effort of UNSW and to develop their research expertise through the pursuit of defined projects relevant to their particular field of research. This position is part of the project to develop high efficiency chalcogenide thin film solar cells and related tandem solar cells. The role of Post-Doctoral Fellow reports to Scientia Prof Xiaojing Hao and has no direct reports. Salary, Level A - $122,592 per annum + 17% superannuation Full Time: 35 hours Fixed-term contract for 12 months Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Faculty of Engineering at UNSW continues to be a national and international leader in engineering research and education, cementing its status as the University’s premier research faculty. As the largest Engineering Faculty in Australia, it graduates the highest number of engineers and maintains the largest research expenditure in the country. Through excellence in both fundamental and applied research, the Faculty strives to sustain and further its global impact, aiming to be ranked among the top engineering faculties worldwide. For more information, visit the Faculty website: https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/about-us Skills & Experience: A PhD in Photovoltaic Engineering, Materials Engineering, Physics, or a related field, with research directly relevant to next-generation solar cell design and characterisation. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills in chalcogenide thin film solar cells and tandem solar cells, spanning fundamental research, device fabrication and performance optimisation. Extensive hands-on experience in the basic characterisation of compound semiconductor materials, thin film solar cell devices, including but not limited to, electrical, optical, and morphological characterisation. Proven experience in developing advanced characterisation techniques for thin-film solar cells and tandem solar cells, either using existing method to characterise new properties or combined different technologies for complicated characterisation. Demonstrated ability to undertake high quality academic research and conduct independent research with limited supervision. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated track record of publications and conference presentations relative to opportunity. Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for these positions can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW . To Apply: Please click the apply now button and submit your CV, Cover Letter and Responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: Allyssar Hamoud – Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Wednesday 21 st October 2026 UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543065/senior-health-safety-advisor-management-systems": {
      "title": "Senior Health & Safety Advisor- Management Systems",
      "description": "Senior Health & Safety Advisor- Management Systems Apply now Job no: 543065 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Risk & Safety Employment Type: Full time, Fixed term Duration: 2 years Remuneration: $132,445 - $149,228 + 17% super Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW is embarking on a significant transformation of its Health & Safety function, with a focus on strengthening systems, governance, risk management, assurance, compliance, and data-driven decision making across the University. As part of this transformation, we are seeking an experienced Senior Health & Safety Advisor - Management Systems to play a key role in designing, implementing and continuously improving our Health & Safety Management System. Reporting to the Senior Health & Safety Manager - Systems, this role will provide enterprise-wide health and safety expertise focused on maintaining an ISO 45001-aligned management system, improving safety performance through digital transformation, and strengthening risk and assurance practices across the University. You will partner with stakeholders across a diverse and complex environment to embed best practice safety processes, influence positive safety outcomes, and support strategic organisational change. Your focus: Lead the design, maintenance and continuous improvement of UNSW's Health & Safety Management Systems, including standards, procedures, guidance materials and tools. Drive the ongoing alignment of systems and processes with ISO 45001 principles and industry best practice. Lead initiatives to improve health and safety reporting, analytics, dashboards and data governance, ensuring high-quality insights support decision-making. Partner with stakeholders to identify opportunities for digital transformation and continuous improvement across health and safety systems and processes. Provide expert advice on health and safety legislation, risk management, governance and assurance requirements. Support complex incident investigations, risk assessments, corrective actions and control verification activities. Monitor enterprise risk trends and provide recommendations to strengthen risk controls and improve organisational performance. Coordinate assurance activities, audits and certification requirements, including engagement with regulators and external providers as required. Provide coaching and technical support to leaders and stakeholders to strengthen risk management capability and safety outcomes. Collaborate across Health & Safety, Wellbeing, Operations and other functional teams to integrate physical and psychosocial risk management into business practices. Who You Are: You are an experienced health and safety professional with a strong background in management systems and continuous improvement. You thrive in complex environments, combining technical expertise and stakeholder engagement to deliver practical, sustainable safety outcomes. You will hold the following skills & experience: Relevant tertiary qualification, or equivalent experience gained through a combination of education, training and significant health and safety experience. Demonstrated experience developing, implementing and improving Health & Safety Management Systems within a large or complex organisation. Strong knowledge of health and safety legislation, ISO 45001 standards and contemporary industry best practice. Experience working with health and safety systems, data management, analytics, reporting and performance dashboards. Proven ability to interpret complex legislative, regulatory and organisational requirements and translate them into practical and user-friendly processes and documentation. Excellent stakeholder engagement and relationship management skills, with the ability to influence outcomes across all levels of an organisation. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and deliver outcomes in a fast-paced environment. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, with a high-level attention to detail. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the position description (found on job listing on JOBS@UNSW). How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter (required) detailing your interest and suitability for the job before Sunday 25th October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December shutdown period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: For queries on the recruitment process, please contact Rachel Darcy: r.darcy@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contacts above cannot be accepted . #LI-KM1 Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543199/senior-program-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Program Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Program Analyst Apply now Job no: 543199 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management, Analyst Employment Type: Full Time, Fixed Term 2-year opportunity. Remuneration: Level 8: from $132,445+ 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington with transition to newly renovated office at our Randwick campus, anticipated for October 2026 About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About the Role The Senior Program Analyst will play a key role in the successful delivery of the Business Optimisation Strategy (BOS). The Senior Program Analyst supports the PMO function for the BOS program by providing integrated reporting, data analysis, and governance insights across all workstreams. The role provides and supports the PMO’s single source of truth for program performance, enabling informed decision-making through a consolidated view of program health and detailed analysis of workstream-level performance across schedule, risks, resources, and financials. The Senior Program Analyst will report to the Senior PMO Lead and has no direct reports. While allocated to the program, the Senior Program Analyst will liaise heavily with the Program Managers for daily work allocations. Responsibilities Lead the consolidation and analysis of data across all BOS workstreams to deliver integrated reporting on schedules, risks, dependencies, resources, and financials. Maintain program-wide governance artefacts, including risks, issues, actions, and decisions, ensuring they are traceable, up to date, and aligned with PMO standards. Support integrated planning by monitoring milestones, critical path activities, and cross-stream dependencies to identify risks and recommend corrective actions. Maintain data integrity and consistency across all reporting tools and outputs, upholding the PMO as the single source of truth. Prepare high-quality reporting packs, insights, and analysis for SteerCo, PMG, and other governance forums to drive informed decision-making. Evaluate program performance data to identify trends, variances, and emerging risks, escalating critical matters and advising leadership on strategic adjustments. Translate complex program, project, and financial data into clear, actionable insights for key stakeholders at all levels. Coordinate cross-workstream PMO operations to ensure alignment across delivery activities, governance processes, and reporting cadences. About You Tertiary qualifications or equivalent substantial experience, paired with a practical understanding of modern project management methodologies in fast-paced program environments. Advanced proficiency in Power BI to build dynamic dashboards, standardise governance reporting, and deliver ad hoc analysis for senior stakeholder forums. Strong analytical, problem-solving, and critical thinking skills, with high attention to detail in policy interpretation and complex data analysis. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, with a proven ability to build strong stakeholder relationships, influence outcomes, and translate complex insights clearly. Demonstrated initiative and ability to work both autonomously and collaboratively, with a commitment to alignment with organizational values, policies, and objectives. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before 11:55pm Sunday 25 October 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543541/strategic-education-projects-manager-science": {
      "title": "Strategic Education Projects Manager - Science",
      "description": "Strategic Education Projects Manager - Science Apply now Job no: 543541 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The Strategic Education Projects Manager plays a leading role within the Faculty of Science in the planning, development, implementation and evaluation of strategic education, curriculum, assessment and student experience initiatives across the Faculty. The role provides high-level project leadership and specialist expertise to complex educational projects that strengthen learning and teaching, enhance curriculum quality, and support innovation across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. The role is responsible for managing and delivering significant educational initiatives that contribute to Faculty and University strategic priorities, ensuring projects are effectively planned, implemented and evaluated to achieve intended outcomes, with a focus on improving educational quality, student outcomes and organisational capability. Working across multiple portfolios, the Strategic Education Projects Manager influences and supports educational innovation, curriculum transformation, quality enhancement, governance activities and continuous improvement to support the successful delivery of Faculty initiatives as well as locally implemented University initiatives. The Strategic Education Projects Manager works in close partnership with the Deputy Dean (Education), Associate Deans (Education), Heads of School, academic leaders and professional staff across the Faculty of Science and the Science Education Team to shape educational strategy and enable the successful implementation of learning and teaching priorities. The role builds collaborative partnerships across the University, provides expert advice on educational policy and practice, and contributes to a culture of innovation, evidence-informed decision-making and educational excellence. About the role $132k - $149k plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Continuing Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Lead the planning, development, implementation and evaluation of strategic education, curriculum, assessment and student experience initiatives that advance the Faculty of Science's educational priorities and align with University strategy. Develop project strategies, implementation plans, business cases, evaluation frameworks and supporting documentation to enable the successful delivery and continuous improvement of educational initiatives. Lead curriculum transformation initiatives that strengthen program coherence, assessment design, graduate employability, professional identity development, work-integrated learning and contemporary approaches to learning and teaching. Provide strategic advice and recommendations to the Deputy Dean (Education), Associate Deans (Education), Faculty leadership and project sponsors on educational priorities, project delivery, policy, emerging risks and opportunities. Ensure projects comply with University governance, policy, quality assurance and regulatory requirements, maintaining consistent and effective project management methodologies and reporting practices. Lead cross-functional project teams and working groups, fostering collaboration, accountability and shared ownership to deliver sustainable educational outcomes. Build capability across the Faculty by supporting academic leaders and educators to implement contemporary educational practice, fostering a culture of innovation, collaboration, scholarly teaching and continuous improvement. Develop and maintain productive relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including central divisions, professional accreditation bodies, government agencies and sector partners where appropriate. Prepare high-quality reports, discussion papers, submissions and presentations for Faculty and University committees, providing evidence-informed advice to support strategic decision-making. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Ensure hazards and risks psychosocial and physical are identified and controlled for tasks, projects, and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. About the successful applicant (Selection criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A relevant qualification in education, higher education, learning design, organisational leadership or a related discipline, with extensive relevant experience; or an equivalent level of knowledge, skills and experience gained through a combination of education, training and professional practice. Demonstrated experience leading strategic education initiatives within higher education, including the development, implementation and evaluation of complex educational, curriculum, assessment or student success projects. Extensive knowledge of contemporary higher education pedagogy, curriculum design, assessment, quality assurance, student learning and educational innovation, with the ability to translate evidence into effective institutional practice. Demonstrated understanding of higher education governance, policy, quality assurance and regulatory frameworks, with experience applying these within curriculum and educational initiatives. Proven experience leading organisational change and managing complex projects involving multiple stakeholders, competing priorities and significant institutional impact. Excellent relationship management, consultation and stakeholder engagement skills, with a demonstrated ability to build trusted partnerships, facilitate collaboration and influence without direct authority. Highly developed analytical and problem-solving skills, with experience using evidence, data and evaluation to inform strategic planning, decision-making and continuous improvement. Outstanding written and verbal communication skills, including demonstrated experience preparing strategic reports, briefing papers, committee papers, business cases and executive-level presentations. Excellent organisational and project management skills, with the ability to manage multiple complex initiatives simultaneously while exercising sound judgement, initiative and strategic thinking. Demonstrated proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and experience using project management, collaboration, curriculum management and data reporting systems relevant to higher education. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW's values, strategic priorities, and relevant policies and procedures. Knowledge of health and safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and a commitment to maintaining a safe and healthy workplace. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. For informal queries, please see the below contact details. Otherwise, please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed . Contact: Tracey Kuit E: t.kuit@unsw.edu.au Applications close: November 3 rd , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543542/education-development-manager-science": {
      "title": "Education Development Manager - Science",
      "description": "Education Development Manager - Science Apply now Job no: 543542 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Student Services One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The Education Development Manager plays a key role within the Faculty of Science by providing leadership and strategic thinking in the development and implementation of innovative learning practices. The role entails leading the Educational Development team to design and develop quality learning activities, modules, programs and courses in collaboration with academic staff to support local implementation of learning and teaching strategies across the Faculty of Science. About the role $132k - $149k plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Continuing Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Lead and develop a high-performing Education Development team, providing clear direction, coaching and performance management to build capability, foster engagement and deliver high-quality educational outcomes. Provide strategic leadership for education development across the Faculty, acting as a key liaison with Schools, Faculty stakeholders and central university teams on systems, initiatives and priorities that support learning and teaching. Represent the Faculty of Science on relevant University committees, working groups, workshops and forums, contributing expertise and ensuring Faculty priorities and perspectives are effectively represented. Partner with academic staff to design, develop and enhance undergraduate and postgraduate curriculum, ensuring learning activities, assessment and feedback are aligned, inclusive, accessible and informed by effective pedagogical practice. Lead the design and development of high-quality learning activities, modules, programs and courses, applying evidence-informed approaches to improve student engagement, learning outcomes and the overall quality of teaching across the Faculty. Provide expert advice and consultation to academic and professional staff on curriculum design, educational technologies, teaching practice and the implementation of innovative and effective approaches to learning and teaching. Build and maintain strong relationships with key stakeholders across Schools, the Faculty and the wider University, facilitating collaboration and supporting the successful implementation of Faculty and University education strategies. Lead the development of reporting, evaluation and continuous improvement processes, providing high-quality analysis, insights and recommendations to support decision-making and educational priorities, including those of the Deputy Dean (Education). Provide high-level strategic and policy advice to the Deputy Dean (Education), Associate Deans, Faculty leadership and School teaching committees on emerging issues, educational priorities and opportunities for improvement. Develop and oversee effective stakeholder communications, ensuring education development initiatives, projects, priorities and outcomes are communicated clearly and consistently to relevant internal and external audiences. Contribute to the planning and implementation of Faculty-wide education initiatives and projects, ensuring activities are appropriately scoped, prioritised and delivered in alignment with strategic objectives. Champion a culture of innovation, collaboration and continuous improvement in learning and teaching, identifying emerging practices and opportunities that enhance the student and staff experience. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Ensure hazards and risks psychosocial and physical are identified and controlled for tasks, projects, and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. About the successful applicant (Selection criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: Relevant degree with extensive relevant experience, or equivalent competency gained through any other combination of education, training or experience in a higher education organisation. Extensive experience in educational design and/or development, including end-to-end course design following constructive alignment principles and a strong understanding of the Technology-Enabled Learning and Teaching (TELT) and Universal Design for Learning (UDL) landscape. Well-developed leadership and management skills with the ability to motivate and manage staff through change and ambiguity. Demonstrated experience in driving innovation and process improvement initiatives in a complex environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Sound stakeholder management skills, with the ability to influence senior management and liaise effectively with a range of stakeholders to ensure successful outcomes. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. For informal queries, please see the below contact details. Otherwise, please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed . Contact: Elly Pineda E: e.pineda@unsw.edu.au Applications close: November 3 rd , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543560/education-developer-science": {
      "title": "Education Developer - Science",
      "description": "Education Developer - Science Apply now Job no: 543560 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Student Services One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The Education Developer plays a key role within the Faculty of Science by designing and developing high-quality learning resources, curriculum, assessment, workshops and educational processes that support effective and innovative learning and teaching. The role provides specialist educational advice and contributes to Faculty strategic priorities, including through expertise in one or more priority areas of educational practice. The role works collaboratively with academic and professional staff to design, develop and enhance learning activities, resources, assessments, curricula, modules, programs and courses, supporting the implementation of Faculty and University learning and teaching strategies and strategic priorities. NOTE: THERE ARE x3 ROLES AVAILABLE. About the role $118k - $128k plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Continuing Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Design, develop and enhance high-quality learning and teaching resources, including online and blended learning materials, learning activities, modules, course resources, staff guidance and supporting documentation, applying evidence-informed educational design principles. Partner with academic staff on curriculum and course design and renewal, providing specialist educational advice to support coherent curriculum structures, constructive alignment, student engagement and achievement of course and program learning outcomes. Provide specialist advice on assessment design and practice, including authentic assessment, assessment for learning, feedback strategies, academic integrity, inclusive assessment and appropriate use of educational technologies. Design and facilitate professional learning workshops, communities of practice and other capability-building activities for academic and professional staff on contemporary approaches to learning, teaching, assessment and curriculum design. Develop, document and improve education development processes, frameworks, templates, guidelines and resources that enable consistent, efficient and high-quality approaches to learning and teaching across the Faculty. Contribute to the implementation of Faculty and University education strategies and strategic priorities, translating strategic objectives into practical initiatives, resources, processes and approaches that can be adopted by Schools and teaching teams. Lead or contribute to defined education development projects and initiatives, coordinating activities, stakeholders, timelines and deliverables and providing specialist input to achieve agreed outcomes. Provide consultation and specialist educational advice to academic and professional staff, identifying appropriate pedagogical approaches and solutions based on the educational context, student cohort, delivery mode and intended learning outcomes. Apply inclusive and accessible education design principles to the development of curriculum, assessment, learning activities and resources, supporting equitable participation and learning outcomes for diverse student cohorts. Evaluate the effectiveness of educational initiatives, resources and practices, using student, staff and learning data, feedback and relevant evidence to inform recommendations and continuous improvement. Identify, evaluate and support the adoption of emerging approaches, tools and practices in higher education, contributing to innovation and continuous improvement in learning and teaching. Build effective working relationships with Schools, academic staff, professional teams and relevant University stakeholders, contributing educational expertise to collaborative projects, working groups and communities of practice. Contribute to reporting, evaluation and communication activities relating to education development projects, strategic initiatives and learning and teaching priorities. Maintain contemporary knowledge of higher education pedagogy, educational research and sector developments, applying relevant evidence and good practice to educational development activities. Develop and maintain recognised specialist expertise in at least one Faculty or University priority area, providing advice, resources, professional development and project support within that area. Priority specialisations may include: educational technology and technology-enhanced learning Universal Design for Learning (UDL), accessibility and inclusive education work-integrated learning and experiential learning career development learning and employability assessment and feedback academic integrity and assessment security artificial intelligence in learning, teaching and assessment curriculum and program design online, blended and hybrid learning learning analytics and evidence-informed educational improvement student transition, belonging and retention Indigenous curriculum and pedagogies intercultural learning and internationalisation of the curriculum Sustainability (SDGs) education authentic, inquiry-based and project-based learning educational evaluation and scholarship of teaching and learning. Act as a Faculty resource and subject matter specialist within the nominated priority area, building capability in others and contributing to Faculty-wide initiatives, guidance and good-practice approaches. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health & safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: Relevant degree with extensive relevant experience, or equivalent competency gained through any other combination of education, training or experience in a higher education organisation. Extensive experience in educational design and/or development, including end-to-end course design following constructive alignment principles and a strong understanding of the Technology-Enabled Learning and Teaching (TELT) and Universal Design for Learning (UDL) landscape. Strong collaboration skills with the ability to support and motivate team members through change and ambiguity. Demonstrated experience in driving innovation and process improvement initiatives in a complex environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail for deliverables produced. Sound stakeholder management skills, with the ability to influence senior management and liaise effectively with a range of stakeholders to ensure successful outcomes. Excellent time management skills, with a demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines by using judgement and initiative. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. For informal queries, please see the below contact details. Otherwise, please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed . Contact: Elly Pineda E: e.pineda@unsw.edu.au Applications close: November 3 rd , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543570/teaching-support-admin-officer-school-of-civil-environmental-engineering": {
      "title": "Teaching Support Admin Officer - School of Civil & Environmental Engineering",
      "description": "Teaching Support Admin Officer - School of Civil & Environmental Engineering Apply now Job no: 543570 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Student Recruitment / Admissions The Opportunity The Teaching Support Administration Officer is a central role in the Education portfolio in Engineering, overseeing the administration of teaching timetables, exams, results, casual academics and general academic support. The role will work closely with the Team Leaders, Academic Program Authorities and Course Convenors to support the administration of undergraduate and postgraduate programs and courses. The role of Teaching Support Administration Officer reports to the School Manager, Civil and Environmental Engineering with a dotted line to the TSA Team Leader and has no direct reports. Salary: Level 5 - $92,265 to $104,401 per annum + 17% superannuation Level 6 - $108,021 to $115,233 per annum + 17% superannuation Employment type: Full Time Appointment type: Fixed Term to December 2027 Location: Kensington – Sydney, Australia Work arrangement: On-site role About Us UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. But what makes us different isn’t only what we do, it’s how we do it. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged, and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Faculty of Engineering at UNSW continues to be a national and international leader in engineering research and education, cementing its status as the University’s premier research faculty. As the largest Engineering Faculty in Australia, it graduates the highest number of engineers and maintains the largest research expenditure in the country. Through excellence in both fundamental and applied research, the Faculty strives to sustain and further its global impact, aiming to be ranked among the top engineering faculties worldwide. For more information, visit the Faculty website: https://www.unsw.edu.au/engineering/about-us Skills and Experience Level 5 Responsible for Term Planning and Class Maintenance activities including timetabling, class scheduling, clash free course combinations (CFCCs), creation of Moodle courses and relevant room bookings. Deliver consistent, accurate and customer focused advice to solve diverse teaching/education related enquiries, referring to UNSW policy and procedures as required. Process casual academic recruitment through Unihire and oversee processes relating to their onboarding and payment. Responsible for undergraduate and postgraduate coursework thesis support to Thesis Coordinators in Schools, including the organisation of submissions, seminars and poster sessions. Act as the administrative contact for Astra, performing quality assurance checks on grades prior to submission and coordinating the approval of final grades with Assessment Review Committees to ensure all grades are entered in a timely manner. Support with the implementation and evaluation of learning, teaching and assessment programs within the Faculty of Engineering. Provide administrative support to Academic Learning and Teaching Coordinators including reviewing and updating admission check sheets and producing and maintaining requisite flow charts. Respond to requests from academic staff and provide proactive advice and solution-focused action in a timely and accurate manner. Responsible for monitoring enrolments and provision of enrolment information to Schools including but not limited to: Reporting on class utilisation and recommendations for additional or reduction of classes; Using enrolment patterns to monitor and forecast future enrolment numbers; and Assisting Schools in making term planning decisions about class sizes, class numbers and casual academic hiring. Responsible for assessment planning, including collating assessment planning data and exam papers from academics, facilitating academic review of assessment tasks, creating internal timetables, coordinating online and distance examinations. Level 6 (in addition to the above) Significant relevant experience in a similar role in a tertiary organisation. Experience providing a broad range of teaching administration support and advice to Academic staff. Demonstrated ability to apply, interpret, advise on and contribute to the development of policies, systems, manuals, rules, procedures and guidelines. As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Additional details about the specific responsibilities for these positions can be found in the position description. This is available via JOBS@UNSW. To Apply: Please click the apply now button and submit your CV, Cover Letter and Responses to the Skills and Experience. You should systematically address the Skills and Experience listed within the position description in your application. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Please note applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed below. Contact: Allyssar Hamoud – Talent Acquisition Associate E: a.hamoud@unsw.edu.au Applications close: 11:55 pm (Sydney time) on Tuesday 20 th October 2026 UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543172/administrator-research-surgical-and-orthopaedic-research-laboratory-sorl": {
      "title": "Administrator (Research) - Surgical and Orthopaedic Research Laboratory (SORL)",
      "description": "Administrator (Research) - Surgical and Orthopaedic Research Laboratory (SORL) Apply now Job no: 543172 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Research Administration support Employment Type: Full Time, 35 hours per week Duration: 12 months fixed term (Parental Leave Cover) Remuneration: Professional Level 5 $92,265 plus 17% super Location: Kensington, New South Wales Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Candidates must hold unrestricted work rights to be considered for this position. Position Summary The Administrator (Research) provides high-quality, day-to-day research administration support to the Surgical and Orthopaedic Research Laboratory (SORL), with a particular focus on supporting post-award grant activities, reporting requirements and the effective administration of active research projects. Working closely with researchers and relevant University stakeholders, the role helps ensure research activities are well coordinated, compliant with grant requirements and supported by efficient administrative processes. This is a research-focused administrative role within a non-standard laboratory environment, requiring a high degree of flexibility, adaptability and attention to detail. The Administrator will manage a broad range of activities including research administration, record and database management, meetings and events, correspondence, staff and visitor onboarding, and project and process improvement activities. The role requires someone who is proactive and organised, understands when matters require follow-up, and can exercise sound judgement to ensure actions and deadlines do not fall through the gaps. Strong interpersonal and communication skills are essential, as the position works with researchers, professional staff and internal and external stakeholders with different working styles, priorities and personalities. The successful candidate will be comfortable building effective working relationships, adapting their approach to different stakeholders and responding positively to changing priorities and requirements within a dynamic research environment. The role requires strong organisational and problem-solving skills, advanced numeracy and Microsoft Office capability, and the ability to work independently while contributing collaboratively to the broader research team. A practical, responsive approach and the ability to remain organised and accurate while adapting to changing circumstances will be important to success in the position. The role reports to the Professor of Surgery and has no direct reports. Some Key Skills Required: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Advanced time management, organisational and problem-solving skills, including the ability to meet deadlines. Well-developed interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated experience providing effective customer service and support to clients at all levels. Highly proficient computer literacy with excellent skills in Microsoft Office applications. Ability to make sound judgements and work both independently and as part of a team. Demonstrated experience supporting and contributing to projects and initiatives. Advanced numeracy skills. Please refer to the position description for full details. Benefits and Culture: UNSW offer a competitive salary and access to a plethora of UNSW-perks including: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments 10 days paid cultural leave per year Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) More information on the great staff benefits and culture can be found here How to Apply: Submit your application including your resume & selection criteria online before Wednesday 14th October at 11:55pm. To be considered for this role, you must address the Selection Criteria which are outlined in the “Skills and Experience” section of the position description. Applications that do not address these criteria will not be considered Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Aarti a.aarti@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Associate Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543421/technical-officer": {
      "title": "Technical Officer",
      "description": "Technical Officer Apply now Job no: 543421 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Research Infrastructure One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. The School of Biological, Earth and Environmental Sciences (BEES) examines the natural environment, including life, from the core of the Earth to its upper atmosphere, deep in time through to the present and modelling towards the future. Research within BEES is clustered within four general thematic areas: Ecology and Evolutionary Biology; Climate Science; Environmental Change, Sustainability and Resources; Paleoenvironments and Earth History. The Technical Officer supports the efficient operation of teaching and research laboratories in the School, by providing high quality professional and technical support. The role involves managing laboratory activities, ensuring compliance with WHS standards, and contributing to continuous improvement. Working with a high degree of autonomy, the Technical Officer supports undergraduate courses, coordinates lab operations, and provides on site supervision of casual staff as needed. With a strong focus on Workplace Health and Safety, the Technical Officer works with the team to ensure compliance with WHS requirements, managing WHS activities and implementing policies and procedures. About the role Level 6 - $108K - $115K plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Continuing Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Plan for and oversee day-to-day management and scheduling of teaching and/or research laboratories and activities to facilitate the delivery of laboratory teaching and/or research. Provide quality technical support to the shared research and/or teaching laboratories, ensuring the timely preparation and turnaround of equipment and learning materials (physical and/or online) for practical classes. Provide authoritative technical and logistical advice to staff and students, including demonstration of techniques, operation of equipment, and responding to technical requests in a timely and courteous manner. Procure research and teaching materials, including field collection of materials. Manage the safe and efficient use of research equipment and infrastructure, ensuring appropriate access and induction, training of students and staff, troubleshooting, routine maintenance, repair, and documentation. Develop and implement processes to improve the coordination of teaching and/or research activities to facilitate the delivery of laboratory teaching and/or research. Support undergraduate fieldwork, by organising logistics such as accommodation, transport and food, and participate in fieldwork activities as required. Collaborate with academic staff to develop and enhance teaching (e.g. lab practicals, field trips) and/or research methods within the School, including laboratory teaching and/or research, equipment, infrastructure, services, and training. Collaborate with academic staff and laboratory users to ensure laboratory services align with teaching and research needs. Contribute to a culture of continuous improvement by evaluating existing equipment, infrastructure, service delivery and training processes, and identify opportunities for improvement and implement changes in consultation with the Technical Laboratory Manager and relevant stakeholders. Manage and maintain the following facilities and systems in BEES (where appropriate): o Vehicle fleet, include trailers, cold storage facilities, and inventory systems. Teaching microscopes. Rock storage and operating the rock crushing facilities. Glasshouse, John T. Waterhouse Herbarium. Organise procurement, purchasing, asset management, stock control and safe disposal of equipment and laboratory consumables. Work closely with the BEES HSE Advisor to address WHS issues including risk assessment; development and implementation of Safe Work Procedures and other safety documents; monitoring compliance of WHS practices and corrective actions; ensuring high standards of laboratory work practices are maintained. Provide advice and training on WHS issues, ensuring compliance of users prior to granting laboratory access, and ongoing supervision and monitoring of staff, students and visitors as required. Perform other appropriate duties and responsibilities as assigned by the Technical Laboratory Manager. Act as the first aid officer and undertake relevant training and refreshers as required.  Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A relevant tertiary qualification and subsequent experience in a similar role or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of education, training and/or experience. Extensive understanding of Geosciences and/or Biological Sciences and/or experience working in a teaching and/or research laboratory. Knowledge of workplace health and safety and experience implementing safety processes in a laboratory environment. Demonstrated experience in coordinating and participating in fieldwork activities in a similar discipline. Ability to manage fieldwork logistics, including accommodation, transport, food and equipment preparation. Proficiency in operating, maintaining, and managing scientific equipment. Experience with IT based laboratory management systems and analysing, investigating and troubleshooting technical issues. Experience in identifying process improvement and implementing innovative solutions. Effective record keeping skills (both paper and electronic), including the ability to work accurately, with strong attention to detail. Excellent time management and organisational skills including the ability to prioritise work effectively. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills (both verbal and written) to effectively engage with a diverse range of stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to apply, interpret, advise on the development of policies, systems, procedures and guidelines. Ability to handle commercially sensitive information with discretion and professionalism. Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and contribute positively to the effective working of a team. A current first aid certificate or a willingness to obtain one to undertake first aid officer duties. A valid and current driver’s licence and an ability or willingness to learn to drive vehicles with trailers and minibuses. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. For informal queries, please see the below contact details. Otherwise, please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent directly to the contact listed . Contact: Frank Hemmings E: f.hemmings@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 18 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542919/lead-solution-architect-data": {
      "title": "Lead Solution Architect - Data",
      "description": "Lead Solution Architect - Data Apply now Job no: 542919 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: Full Time, Fixed Term 12-month opportunity Remuneration: Total Remuneration Package Location: Kensington, NSW About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About the Role UNSW IT is supporting the Business Optimisation Strategy (BOS) transformation program to overhaul Finance, People & Culture, and IT service delivery through new Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) and Enterprise Service Management (ESM) platforms. This initiative involves migrating data across more than 80 impacted applications, decommissioning at least 16 legacy systems, and establishing compliant data archiving and retention processes. The Lead Solution Architect - Data provides technical oversight for data migration, application impacts, and solution design across the BOS program. Dual-aligned to the Enterprise Data Office under Andrew and the IT stream, the role ensures data governance, retention compliance, and integration with the UNSW Enterprise Data Intelligence Platform. Key responsibilities include overseeing data migration from legacy systems like PeopleSoft into the new Oracle platforms, assessing application impacts, designing pragmatic data solutions, and guiding engineers and testers. The position defines data retention schedules, attachment storage rules, and integration architectures between new platforms and enterprise reporting, while collaborating closely with BOS Program Managers, Enterprise Architects, and the Enterprise Data Office to maintain architectural quality and governance across all data initiatives. Responsibilities Lead the data solution outcomes for the BOS Program, partnering with senior stakeholders to translate complex requirements into endorsed target state architectures and transition roadmaps. Evaluate existing data platforms to identify necessary changes, recommending pragmatic and cost-effective implementations across cloud, SaaS, and on-premises environments. Produce high-quality architectural artefacts, including current and target state designs, options assessments, decision records, and reusable design patterns. Collaborate with enterprise architects, cybersecurity, and delivery teams to ensure data solutions are cohesive, scalable, resilient, and aligned with UNSW enterprise direction. Define and deliver data archiving and records retention frameworks that comply with all legislative, regulatory, and university policy requirements. Drive architecture and security governance by assuring solution designs, managing risks, and presenting recommendations to boards such as the Architecture Review Board. About You Proven track record as a Data Solution Architect leading ERP or similar large-scale transformation programs within complex enterprise environments. Deep experience architecting cloud, SaaS, and on-premises data solutions, with strong knowledge of Oracle Fusion ERP, Lakehouse architecture, and hybrid integration patterns. Expertise in embedding data governance, privacy, security, and regulatory compliance into architectural designs and organisational frameworks. Strong ability to establish data standards, modelling patterns, and reference architectures across metadata, quality, and trusted data sharing. Demonstrated capability to evaluate complex technical options, balance delivery risks and costs, and deliver pragmatic recommendations to executive stakeholders. Outstanding communication and negotiation skills to articulate complex concepts, influence senior leadership, and align competing business priorities. Relevant tertiary qualifications or equivalent industry experience, supported by exceptional planning, problem-solving, and multi-stream prioritisation skills. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the job description ) before Sunday 18 th October 2026 by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Job queries contact: Tai James, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: tai.james@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543096/senior-data-scientist": {
      "title": "Senior Data Scientist",
      "description": "Senior Data Scientist Apply now Job no: 543096 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Analyst Employment Type: Full Time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 8 from $132k + 17% superannuation & leave loading Location:  Kensington NSW Why your role matters The Senior Data Scientist holds a key role within the Educational Data & Insights team and is responsible for leveraging advanced data science and AI to ensure our university makes the most of student feedback to improve learning and teaching. The Educational Data & Insights team at UNSW manages 2 major survey programs for the university. This includes the MyExperience course-level survey, as well as the Australia-wide and government-funded Quality Indicators for Learning and Teaching (QILT) program. These datasets combine quantitative and qualitative student responses and are paramount for UNSW to develop a greater understanding of its diverse students. The qualitative responses in particular represent an extremely rich dataset since they relate to learning and teaching: some of the most complex and multi-faceted experiences we undergo in our lives. Working closely with our Manager – Educational Data & Insights, our Senior Analytics Engineer, our Research Data Scientist and our Educational Evaluation Specialist, this role is an incredible opportunity to develop and have a significant impact at one of Australia’s leading and most diverse universities. Focusing primarily on our myExperience Reporting Solution (one of the university’s most utilised data products) and leading the development of Thematic Analysis tools and Machine Learning models, this role delivers insights to highly engaged academics that are eager for rigorous, evidence-based recommendations. Joining UNSW is your chance to work with leading data tools and technologies in an environment that offers flexible working arrangements and that invests in your development. We are open to various skillsets and fields of study, with a focus on strong data science and programming skills. If you’re passionate about the role and bring transferable skills, we encourage you to apply. This role primarily focuses on the development of Thematic Analysis tools and Machine Learning models (leveraging LLMs and transformers): Conduct rigorous experimentation, hypothesis testing, and deep dive exploratory analyses leveraging advanced statistics to enable our university to make the most of student feedback data and improve its student experience and education outcomes. Lead the development, evaluation and continuous improvement of high-impact Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning tools and automations (in particular Thematic Analysis Solutions leveraging LLMs and transformers). Coach junior colleagues to help lift the broader analytics capability of the team: reviewing their methodology, analysis, code and results through peer reviews. Stay abreast of the latest advancements in Generative AI and data science, identifying opportunities for their application to contribute to our broader university strategy. About You: Essential: Tertiary qualifications in a relevant discipline (s.a. Computer Science, Statistics, Data Science, AI, Computational Linguistics, etc.) or demonstrated relevant work experience. A PhD would be highly regarded. We are also highly interested in individuals who have worked at the crossing of programming/data science and Education, Psychology and Social Sciences or have direct teaching experience. Advanced Programming Skills (Python, SQL) including experience with Python data science libraries (e.g. TensorFlow, PyTorch, scikit-learn, Pandas, NumPy). Demonstrated experience in the development, deployment, evaluation and continuous improvement of Machine Learning and GenAI models. Demonstrated advanced understanding of statistical analysis and machine learning with the ability to justify methods, not just run models. High attention to detail with the ability to assess and document data quality, as well as a track record of conducting data investigations and exploratory analyses. Ability to handle complexity and bring structure: confident in breaking down complex problems, developing strong data architecture, building solutions from scratch, and working with limited direction. Experience maintaining data pipelines, working with cloud-based analytics platforms such as Azure Databricks. Excellent written and verbal communication skills with the ability to communicate complex concepts to diverse audiences. Demonstrated ability to manage competing priorities and deliver high-quality outcomes in a complex environment. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Desirable: Experience with Power BI or similar business intelligence platforms. Experience with Power Platform automation, workflows and Copilot Studio agents. A genuine interest in understanding the specificities of teaching and learning evaluation and of the role of student feedback data. Knowledge of responsible AI practices, data governance and ethical use of data and AI technologies. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working options. Career development opportunities. 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments. Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness). For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter addressing the key selection criteria (no more than 2 pages) before 11.55pm Sunday 18th October 2026. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Job queries contact: Chloe Gane, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: chloe.gane@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543346/project-operations-administrator-student-experience": {
      "title": "Project & Operations Administrator, Student Experience",
      "description": "Project & Operations Administrator, Student Experience Apply now Job no: 543346 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Student Services, Project Management Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 5: $92,265 - $104,401 + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus (flexible work arrangements available; regular on-campus attendance required) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Projects & Operations Administrator provides proactive operational, administrative and project support across a diverse portfolio of student experience initiatives in the Faculty of Arts, Design & Architecture (ADA). Working across projects, events, communications, reporting and systems, the role combines behind-the-scenes coordination with direct engagement and support for students through programs, events and partnership activities. The role contributes to the effective delivery of team priorities while identifying opportunities to improve processes, solve problems and enhance team effectiveness. The role reports to the Manager, Student Experience, works collaboratively across the Student Experience team to support faculty priorities, and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Provide proactive operational, administrative and project support across student experience initiatives, including events, engagement programs, communications, equity initiatives and reporting. Support the coordination, delivery and evaluation of student experience projects, programs and events in collaboration with stakeholders. Administer student experience and equity initiatives, maintaining accurate records and contributing to the effective delivery and evaluation of programs and services. Provide administrative and operational support for meetings, workshops, events and initiatives, including venue bookings, catering, scheduling, logistics, resources and follow-up actions. Support evaluation activities, including data collection, maintenance and analysis, and contribute to reporting, dashboards and tracking tools. Maintain and improve operational systems, tools, workflows, templates and documentation with strong attention to detail. Coordinate communications, presentations, reports and other materials for students, staff and stakeholders across a range of channels and platforms. Support student partnership and student voice initiatives, including student ambassador programs, clubs and societies engagement, consultation activities and feedback mechanisms. Provide financial administrative support for day-to-day team operations, including purchasing, payment processing, reconciliations, vendor processes and expenditure tracking. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience, or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience providing proactive administrative, operational and coordination support across multiple projects, initiatives and events, including direct engagement with students and staff. Advanced organisational, time management and problem-solving skills, with a high level of attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities in a dynamic environment. Highly developed written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to prepare communications, reports and presentations and build effective relationships with a broad range of stakeholders. Experience maintaining and improving systems, processes, workflows, documentation and administrative practices to support effective and efficient service delivery. Well-developed computer literacy, including strong skills in Microsoft Office applications, particularly Excel, and confidence in learning and using new technologies and digital platforms. Experience with event management and evaluation software is desirable but not required. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively, exercise initiative, maintain confidentiality and adapt to changing priorities and business needs. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV and a 1-2 page cover letter outlining your suitability for the role addressing each of the selection criteria (listed under ‘skills & experience’ on the PD) via the application portal before 11:30pm on the 15 th of October. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks : As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role . This position will require a Working With Children Check. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543352/senior-case-manager-complex-investigations": {
      "title": "Senior Case Manager- Complex Investigations",
      "description": "Senior Case Manager- Complex Investigations Apply now Job no: 543352 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Governance, Policy & Compliance Employment Type: Full time (35 hours per week) Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 8 $132k-$149k (depending on experience) + 17% flexible super + leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (flexible hybrid work) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why your role matters: In this role, the Senior Case Manager plays a crucial role in managing and investigating some of the University’s most serious and complex cases. You’ll be working with a collaborative team and will be at the forefront of managing and conducting robust investigations into public interest disclosures and other complex misconduct investigations. As an investigator, on the job you will be required to have a working knowledge of the Public Interest Disclosures Act 2022 (NSW), Independent Commission Against Corruption Act 1988 (NSW, Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth)) and willingness to learn and apply further acts as required, to ensure the University meets its legislative and duty of care obligations in managing and investigating the most serious, sensitive, and complex of Conduct & Integrity cases. The role reports to the Head of Disclosures & Complex Investigations and does not have any direct reports. Responsibilities: Core responsibilities for the role include (but are not limited to): Applying a sound understanding of the University’s Codes of Conduct, policies and procedures relating to complaints and allegations of misconduct. Case management of complaints and allegations of misconduct, particularly those of a serious nature. This involves collecting and interpreting evidence, interviewing, drafting correspondence, advising stakeholders, recommending outcomes, preparing reports, providing secretariat support for relevant committees, making inquiries, and maintaining up-to-date records and files in relation to each case. Interpreting and applying complex policy and procedures in relation to the management and investigation of complaints and allegations of misconduct and ensuring that UNSW meets its obligations. Identifying specific or broader organisational issues which may warrant immediate remediation or further review and develop recommendations to address those issues. Skills and Experience: With extensive experience and expert knowledge in workplace investigations, procedural fairness, and the rules of evidence, you’ll bring superior emotional intelligence and resilience alongside exceptional interpersonal and communication skills to the role. Core skills and experience for the role include (but are not limited to): A relevant tertiary qualification and substantial experience in case management of complex misconduct and/or complaints matters, and/or holding an academic or research position. Experience working in complex organisations such as tertiary sector, public service, or large corporate organisations would be highly regarded A Certificate IV in Government Investigations, or willingness to obtain within 12 months of commencement. Demonstrated advanced case management skills, including the proven ability to work in a high volume, process driven administrative environment with multiple deadlines requiring you to work accurately, engage multiple stakeholders and keep contemporaneous records. Demonstrated sound judgment and a superior level of emotional intelligence. Proven ability to exercise initiative, think conceptually and creatively, and to adopt an active and curiosity-driven forensic approach to problem-solving. Demonstrated competence and relevant experience in the analysis and application of regulatory and professional principles, resources and guidelines. Detailed knowledge of procedural fairness, the University’s codes, policies and procedures relating to complaints and allegations of misconduct and current national and international research and case findings. Advanced competence in analysing, developing and writing reports and correspondence with excellent attention to detail. Demonstrated superior advisory, communication, negotiation and interpersonal skills. Further details of the responsibilities and skills and experience can be found in the Position Description on JOBS@UNSW To Apply: Please submit two separate documents: your CV– this should contain your relevant or previous working experience; a cover letter outlining your interest in the role, why you are applying, and how your skills and experience align with the Skills and Experience outlined in the Position Description. Please highlight one or two key strengths you would bring to the team. The closing date for applications is 11:55pm on Wednesday 21 October 2026. Applications sent to either contact below will not be considered. You must apply using the ‘Apply Now’ button below. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role; valid Australian working rights are required on application. Job Queries: Contact Riley O’Keeffe, Head of Disclosures & Complex Investigations at r.okeeffe@unsw.edu.au , or Application questions: Contact Katherine Molloy, Talent Acquisition Partner for any queries regarding the recruitment process at katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au . #LI-KM1 Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working 0ptions Career development opportunities 17% flexible Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits Pre- Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543355/manager-estate-management-portfolio-management-office-pmo": {
      "title": "Manager, Estate Management – Portfolio Management Office (PMO)",
      "description": "Manager, Estate Management – Portfolio Management Office (PMO) Apply now Job no: 543355 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Management Employment Type: Continuing, Full-time, 35 hours per week opportunity Remuneration: Level 9 – from $153,933 plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. About Estate Management Estate Management plays a central role in supporting UNSW’s teaching, research and community mission by planning, developing and maintaining a safe, sustainable and high-performing University estate. With a significant pipeline of capital works, infrastructure, renewal and maintenance initiatives underway, the team is delivering projects that will shape the future learning, research and workplace environment across UNSW’s campuses. Explore UNSW’s current and future campus development projects to learn more about the scale and impact of this work. Why your role matters UNSW is establishing a dedicated Portfolio Management Office to strengthen how we plan, prioritise and deliver a significant program of capital works, infrastructure and maintenance across the University estate. As Manager, Estate Management Portfolio Management Office, you will lead this function and help create the standards, insight and delivery discipline needed to support confident investment and better project outcomes. You will work with senior leaders, project sponsors, delivery teams and governance partners to improve portfolio visibility, strengthen decision-making and build capability across Estate Management. This is an opportunity for an experienced property, infrastructure, capital works or PMO leader to make a visible contribution to a complex and nationally recognised university environment. About the Role The Manager, Estate Management Portfolio Management Office will establish and lead the PMO, bringing greater consistency, transparency and strategic insight to the portfolio. You will help leaders understand performance, investment priorities, emerging risks and delivery capacity, while ensuring projects and maintenance activity are planned and managed through clear, practical standards. Reporting to the Director, Estate Management Operations, you will lead a small team and work across the organisation to influence better decisions, strengthen delivery maturity and support safe, efficient and value-focused outcomes across the University estate. The successful applicant will be required to undertake and have experience in the following areas of expertise: Establish, direct and continuously develop the Estate Management PMO operating model, remit, tools and practices. Set and embed portfolio-wide standards for project planning, cost control, reporting, change management and benefits realisation. Lead portfolio planning and prioritisation processes aligned with Estate Management strategy and funding constraints. Deliver accurate, decision-focused reporting and strategic insights to provide clear visibility over performance, trends and emerging risks. Define and maintain project frameworks and templates aligned with UNSW enterprise requirements, maintaining clear interfaces with governance and assurance functions. Oversee maintenance planning, scheduling and contractor resource allocation to ensure safe, compliant and efficient estate delivery. Build strong stakeholder relationships while fostering a culture of accountability, transparency, disciplined delivery and capability building. About You You are an experienced portfolio, program, property, infrastructure or capital works leader who can bring structure and insight to a complex delivery environment. You are comfortable working at both strategic and operational levels and can turn detailed portfolio information into clear advice for senior decision-makers. Relevant tertiary qualification combined with substantial practical experience, or equivalent professional capability. Experience in property, infrastructure, capital works, facilities, construction or another complex asset environment. Demonstrated senior experience leading a PMO,  portfolio or program delivery function within a complex organisation. Highly developed stakeholder engagement, influencing and communication skills, including the ability to work effectively with senior leaders and executives. Strong expertise in portfolio planning, delivery governance, performance tracking and benefits realisation. Proven track record of scaling project delivery maturity, frameworks and organisational capability. Comprehensive knowledge of the interface between project delivery, governance, risk management and assurance. The ability to manage competing priorities and translate complex portfolio information into clear insights and practical advice. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the Position Description ) before Tuesday 20 October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543381/senior-lead-project-manager-lms-platform": {
      "title": "Senior Lead Project Manager - LMS Platform",
      "description": "Senior Lead Project Manager - LMS Platform Apply now Job no: 543381 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Project Management, Management Employment Type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed-term contract (until June 2029) Remuneration: Competitive Total Fixed Remuneration (TFR) package (TFR = Base salary + 17% Super + Leave Loading) Location: UNSW Kensington Campus – Hybrid work arrangement (2-3 days onsite) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: This is a rare opportunity to lead the platform stream at the heart of UNSW's Learning Management System (LMS) transformation - a strategic investment touching every student, course and academic. You will set the multi-year platform roadmap beyond the life of the program, make high-impact investment and sequencing recommendations, and balance delivery, experience, technical assurance, risk and total cost of ownership across a complex ecosystem. As a senior member of the LMS Program leadership team, you will shape delivery, assurance and governance standards, determine priorities and resource allocation within delegation, and direct architecture, security, data and integration assurance in partnership with UNSW IT. Working across the University, you will author business cases, investment papers and change requests with clear options and trade-offs for executive decision-making. You will influence senior stakeholders, manage competing priorities and deputize for the Program Director as required. Responsibilities Summary: Own end-to-end delivery and release readiness for the LMS platform and ecosystem, including the authority to approve or withhold deployment of capability into production. Lead the strategic alignment, fit ness for purpose, sustainability and total cost of ownership of the LMS Platform Stream across its lifecycle. Set platform stream priorities, manage dependencies and determine practical trade-offs across delivery commitments, technical requirements, budget constraints and organizational priorities. Lead governance and decision-making, approving matters within delegation and presenting roadmap, investment, risk, resourcing and implementation recommendations to the Program Director, Program Steering Committee and senior stakeholders. Develop and maintain the multi-year LMS platform roadmap, identifying future capabilities, investment opportunities and capability gaps through strategic analysis and stakeholder engagement. Author business cases, investment papers and change requests that set out clear options, implications and trade-offs for executive decision-making. Lead resource planning and workforce allocation across the Platform Stream, including staffing, budget and delivery resources within delegation. Own strategic commercial relationships with platform and implementation vendors, including contract performance, service levels, delivery outcomes and future investment recommendations. Monitor business outcomes and benefits, and own the platform's delivery, technical and security risk profile, ensuring effective controls, assurance, data and security obligations and escalation where required. Manage interdependencies with institution-wide programs such as the Academic Calendar Program and Curriculum Transformation, negotiating sequencing and resourcing trade-offs. Build trusted senior stakeholder relationships, resolve competing positions, provide executive reporting and deputize for the Program Director at governance forums when required. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Tertiary qualifications and/or significant experience in similar roles, with substantial proven experience in program and project management. In-depth understanding of the project lifecycle and experience applying PRINCE2, PMBOK, Agile or other recognized project management methodologies. Demonstrated experience leading complex technology programs or delivery streams comprising multiple interdependent projects. Proven ability to influence executive-level stakeholders and governance forums on strategic priorities, investment decisions and delivery approaches. Experience developing business cases, investment recommendations and benefits realization strategies. Demonstrated capability in resource planning, workforce leadership and prioritization in complex organizational environments. Knowledge of budgeting and resource allocation procedures, including experience operating within a formal financial delegation. Advanced written and verbal communication skills, with excellent stakeholder engagement, relationship management and influencing skills. Excellent time management and problem-solving skills, with the judgment and initiative to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW's aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies, guidelines and health and safety responsibilities. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal on Friday, 16 th October, before 11:30pm on the application portal. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries about the recruitment process, please contact the Talent Acquisition Partner, Ayla Kamal on: ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au For queries about the role, please contact the Program Director, Dean MacDonald on: dean.macdonald@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543439/senior-lead-reporting-insights": {
      "title": "Senior Lead, Reporting & Insights",
      "description": "Senior Lead, Reporting & Insights Apply now Job no: 543439 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Analyst Employment Type: Continuing, Full-time, 35 hours per week opportunity Remuneration: Level 9 – from $153,933 plus 17% superannuation and leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid working conditions) About UNSW At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work.  We aspire to be Australia’s global university, improving and transforming lives through excellence in research, outstanding education and a commitment to advancing a just society. Why your role matters UNSW Planning and Performance (UPP) is a trusted strategic partner to university leaders, using business intelligence, forecasting, and performance insights to support better decisions across the University. In the newly created role of Senior Lead, Reporting & Insights, you will shape the strategy, governance, and continuous improvement of UPP’s extensive reporting and dashboard ecosystem. Covering portfolios including student and load forecasting, government reporting, faculty and university performance, and societal impact, you will ensure UPP’s reporting products are purposeful, trusted, accessible, and aligned with organisational priorities. This is an opportunity to bring greater clarity and value to a complex reporting environment. You will lead the review, prioritisation, consolidation, redesign, and retirement of reporting products, helping address dashboard fatigue and ensuring effort is focused on the information that matters most. You will engage with stakeholders across faculties, schools, and professional divisions to understand how decisions are made, translate complex needs into clear priorities, and guide analysts and technical specialists in delivering high-quality, fit-for-purpose insights. Reporting to the Head, Forecasting and Reporting, you will operate with a high degree of autonomy and influence without direct reports. By establishing consistent standards for data integrity, visualisation, usability, and accessibility, you will help strengthen reporting capability across UPP and make a tangible difference to how leaders across UNSW understand performance and act on evidence. About the Role The successful applicant will be required to undertake and have experience in the following areas of expertise: Lead the overall product strategy and governance framework across the university's reporting and dashboard portfolios. Establish governance processes to evaluate, prioritize, maintain, and retire reporting assets effectively. Set consistent standards for usability, data integrity, and best-practice visualisation across all outputs. Monitor portfolio usage and lead initiatives to consolidate, redesign, and streamline reporting tools. Engage key stakeholders across faculties and divisions to translate complex needs into clear reporting specifications. Guide analysts and technical teams in delivering high-quality, user-centred insights and building internal capability. About You Extensive experience delivering reporting and business intelligence solutions using Power BI and tools like Azure Data Factory, Databricks, and complex SQL. Proven track record leading the design, governance, and continuous improvement of reporting products and analytical frameworks in complex environments. Strong conceptual and analytical skills to navigate ambiguity, solve complex problems, and apply sound judgment. Outstanding stakeholder management and communication skills, with the ability to translate complex business needs into clear specifications. Demonstrated experience establishing standards for data governance, accessibility, and universal design in data visualisation. Excellent organizational skills to manage competing priorities, balance strategic and operational demands, and drive user-centred outcomes. Strong alignment with UNSW's aims, objectives, values, and workplace policies. For further information on the role & responsibilities, please refer to the Position Description How to Apply: Submit your CV & cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills & experience bullet points in the Position Description ) before Sunday 25 th October by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December festive period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Position Description Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
