    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543492/learning-design-lead-lms-transformation": {
      "title": "Learning Design Lead - LMS Transformation",
      "description": "Learning Design Lead - LMS Transformation Apply now Job no: 543492 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Employment Type: Full-time (35 hours per week) Duration: Fixed-term contract (till 28 Feb 2030) Remuneration: Competitive Total Fixed Remuneration (TFR) package (TFR = Base salary + 17% Super + Leave Loading) Location: UNSW Kensington Campus – Hybrid work arrangement (2-3 days onsite) About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: UNSW's LMS transformation is a once-in-a-generation opportunity to lift learning across every Faculty. As Learning Design Lead, you will own the learning content migration and uplift that turns a new platform into a coherent, high-quality experience for students and the staff who teach and support them. You will connect LMS delivery with the Academic Calendar and Curriculum Transformation programs, aligning Faculty course redesign, migration priorities and pedagogical innovation so the University moves forward with purpose and consistency. Reporting to the LMS Program Director and leading up to ten direct reports, you will provide strategic educational leadership, set frameworks and templates, and guide a large cross-university program through complexity, competing priorities and change. Your influence will extend from Faculty relationships and course quality to digital learning, teaching and assessment practice, establishing a culture of professionalism, accountability and continuous improvement that endures beyond the transformation. Responsibilities Summary: Lead tactical and strategic projects related to LMS transformation, defining priorities for teaching and the student experience through expert educational leadership and a collaborative, aligned approach. Own the design and implementation of LMS program activities that enable transformation, including frameworks, approaches and templates that support a positive student experience, educational excellence and enhanced learning. Be accountable for the successful migration of every UNSW taught course from every Faculty to the new LMS, aligned with Curriculum Transformation and the pedagogical innovation enabled by the platform. Maximise alignment in Faculty course redesign and development across Curriculum Transformation and LMS migration, supporting consistent standards while respecting the needs of different disciplines and teaching contexts. Own and manage strategic relationships with all Faculties in relation to course migration, building shared understanding of expectations, system use, support, initiatives and opportunities. Lead the development and adoption of innovative practices across the University in digital learning, teaching and assessment to enhance student learning and the student experience. Set strategic guidance for LMS teams and relevant Curriculum Transformation activities, using professional expertise and ongoing dialogue with students and academic colleagues to establish priorities. Establish productive relationships with Schools, Faculties, central units and external stakeholders, keeping partners informed, engaged and knowledgeable about education initiatives and delivery requirements. Set priorities and monitor workflows and systems, providing responsive, outcome-focused guidance to students, Faculties, Schools and central units within agreed service expectations. Lead, manage and develop a team of up to ten staff, creating a collegial, team-based learning environment that delivers high-quality services and stakeholder outcomes. Establish a culture of professionalism, accountability and continuous service improvement across Curriculum Transformation and Flex-semester implementation, and support education initiatives in the Teaching Commons as required. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualifications with a significant and sustained record of teaching expertise and innovation, together with an academic reputation that supports authoritative leadership in education and student experience. Experience establishing, defining and managing learning content development and migration in a Curriculum Transformation and/or LMS migration within a university context. Experience leading strategic projects related to LMS transformation and/or other similar transformational program, including program governance reporting. Proven leadership of innovation in learning and teaching, with demonstrated experience leading multiple staff in a large university and building a high-performing team. Proven ability to create and maintain a collegial, team-based learning approach in higher education that produces high-quality services and stakeholder outcomes. Excellent communication, influencing and negotiation skills, including the ability to build trusted networks with internal and external stakeholders across government and national or international education communities. Demonstrated ability to provide strategic advice, manage competing priorities and make sound judgements across complex, university-wide education initiatives. Enthusiasm for educational opportunities, including those provided by digital innovation, with the ability to translate emerging practice into meaningful improvements in learning and teaching. Demonstrated ability to model UNSW Behaviours, work beyond the boundaries of an individual School or Faculty and build a high-performing organisation based on common values. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW's aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies, guidelines and health and safety responsibilities. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV along with a cover letter addressing your suitability for the role via the application portal on Friday, 16 th October, before 11:30pm on the application portal. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries about the recruitment process, please contact the Talent Acquisition Partner, Ayla Kamal on: ayla.kamal@unsw.edu.au For queries about the role, please contact the LMS Program Director, Dean MacDonald on: dean.macdonald@unsw.edu.au *Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/542263/social-media-student-content-assistant-nura-gili": {
      "title": "Social Media Student Content Assistant, Nura Gili",
      "description": "Social Media Student Content Assistant, Nura Gili Apply now Job no: 542263 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Marketing / Communications At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Ranked among the top 50 universities globally, we attract the highest potential students from Australia and around the world. Our researchers tackle the biggest economic, business, and social challenges with partners around the world. Position Summary A Social Media Student Content Assistant (Level 2) provides support on creating social media content to support Nura Gili marketing activities. This role will: Create original video content that showcases themes of student experience, lifestyle, campus vibrancy and trending topics, featuring in videos/photos where necessary. Assist in the development and delivery of student content for the Nura Gili Instagram, Facebook and LinkedIn platforms, ensuring content is developed in close collaboration with the Nura Gili teams. Contribute to video-based content with a strong, distinctive student voice. Provide additional content support for the ongoing maintenance of the UNSW Indigenous web ecosystem. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. A person engaged is generally expected to have the following skills and experience: Highly motivated and outgoing, with a passion for university life and the educational experience at UNSW. UNSW knowledge including academic, extra-curricular activities, support structures, as well as an understanding of student concerns and cross-cultural issues Good written and verbal communication skills. Awareness of changes and trends on social media platforms. An understanding of in-platform features is desirable but not required. Strong presentation and collaboration skills. The ability to execute video and audio using low-fi technology (e.g. mobile phone). An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Special Requirements: Candidates are required to create a 15-second Instagram Reel on campus in the style of UNSW’s and Nura Gili’s Instagram as part of their application. Additional Pre-employment checks required for this position: Nil Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. If you require any further information regarding the role, please contact David Leslie via David.leslie@unsw.edu.au To submit an application for the role, please do so via the 'Apply Now' button. Please note, no applications will be accepted via email. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 31 Dec 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543312/senior-lecturer-graduate-director-for-our-future-oceans": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer – Graduate Director for Our Future Oceans",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer – Graduate Director for Our Future Oceans Apply now Job no: 543312 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Senior Lecturer One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. THIS ROLE IS LOCATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. The Graduate Director is responsible for leading the development and implementation of an integrated researcher development program at the ARC Centre of Excellence for Our Future Oceans. This program spans five universities: UNSW, ANU, The University of Tasmania, The University of Western Australia and the University of Queensland. The key participants in the researcher development program are PhD students and early to mid-career research associates. The program also extends to honours students and undergraduate students undertaking summer research projects as well as providing opportunities for ongoing mentoring and professional development opportunities for all Centre research staff. The Centre’s integrated program of researcher development activities includes targeted training and workshops, creating mentoring opportunities and establishing linkages for cohorts of young researchers across the Centre, its partner organisations and beyond the university sector. The Graduate Director will ensure that students and postdoctoral researchers gain a world-class ‘value-added’ learning and development experience throughout the candidature or contract duration. Proposed start date February 2027. About the role Level C - (161k – 185k)  - plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 3 years (with possible multi year extension based on performance and funding) Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Lead, develop and implement the Centre’s inter-disciplinary Researcher Development Program in Oceanography. This program includes fundamental scientific research and communication skills, professional development, mentoring and leadership opportunities and will involve all Centre researchers Provide advice and mentoring to students and early career researchers in the Centre of Excellence Design and develop learning activities and resources using a range of suitable approaches and learning environments for post-graduate students and early career researchers Maintain and develop links within the discipline industry and profession (e.g. contribute to professional development activities) Attract quality research students by nurturing and developing an interest and awareness of research in prospective students, research students and postdoctoral fellows Conceptualise, coordinate and run a suite of training and development opportunities for students and early career researchers including scientific winter schools, writing workshops, technical training sessions and sessions that address professional and personal development Establish a research performance culture that fosters and develops research students in order to maintain high PhD completion rates and employment opportunities Develop strategies to recruit and retain students and researchers from under-represented groups Work in collaboration with enrolling institutions to identify student internships and other work integrated learning opportunities for graduate students in the Centre Work collaboratively to identify and apply for additional funding that benefits the Centre of Excellence and the career progression of the Centre’s HDR and ECR cohorts Make a positive contribution by leading Centre meetings and seminars and be a member of Centre committees as required Make independent contributions to research that have a significant impact in their field of expertise and create a nationally recognised research track record. Undertake independent research in discipline or related area (as per the norms of the discipline), making an independent contribution through professional practice and expertise. Develop research groups in area of specialist expertise and research methods that are appropriate to the discipline. Attract peer recognition and establish research network/s (based on the norms of the discipline) at national level. Obtain research income from nationally competitive research grants (and/or research fellowships) and research end-users as a member or leader, at or above the level that is relevant to the discipline in leading universities. Mentor and guide students, groups, and colleagues; supervise HDRs to timely completion as primary supervisor; and contribute to HDR review panels. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant (Selection Criteria) To be successful in this role you will have: A PhD in a related discipline, and/or relevant work experience. Proven commitment to proactively keeping up to date with discipline knowledge and developments. Demonstrated experience in providing mentorship and professional development for HDR students and early career researchers at scale A track record of collegial leadership and a capacity to build and maintain effective collaborative partnerships within and external to the Centre to achieve the mission and objectives of the Centre of Excellence Proven influencing and communication skills to work with and motivate diverse groups of people, both within and outside the University, to achieve agreed goals Experience implementing educational technologies and online delivery methods. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and passion for educational excellence and fostering this in others. Demonstrated success in initiating curriculum development and improvement. Demonstrated experience with industry or work-integrated learning. Evidence of ability to support and inspire students from diverse backgrounds and support student equity diversity and inclusion initiatives. Demonstrated track record in research with outcomes of high quality and high impact with clear evidence of the desire and ability to continually achieve research excellence as well as the capacity for research leadership. Experience in successfully recruiting and supervising high calibre students. Demonstrated ability to work in a team, collaborate across disciplines and build effective relationships. Evidence of highly developed interpersonal and organisational skills. Demonstrated ability to interact with the profession and industry and attract funding for learning and teaching initiatives. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed above in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Matthew England E: m.england@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 29 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543339/research-associate": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate Apply now Job no: 543339 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate One of Australia’s leading research & teaching universities Vibrant campus life with a strong sense of community & inclusion Enjoy a career that makes a difference by collaborating & learning from the best At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. THIS ROLE IS LOCATED IN SYDNEY, AUSTRALIA. The Research Associate (Level A) is expected to contribute towards the Australian Research Council Laureate Fellowship project ‘Caves and their stalagmites: linking climate to groundwater recharge’ led by Professor Andy Baker (FL240100057). They will develop their research expertise through their contribution to a work package led by Prof. Andy Baker (UNSW) and Prof. Andreas Hartmann (TU Dresden) that will use groundwater recharge model simulations to investigate the climate processes that lead to relationships between climate and environmental change and groundwater recharge. The Research Associate will assist the project investigators in carrying out the research project and be responsible for coordinating and undertaking data analysis and drafting papers for publication. The work involves integrating fire events into a global karst recharge model. The effects of wildfire on recharge volumes will be determined for the Black Summer fires, the last 100 yrs; and for future climates and environment change. The Research Associate will also be responsible for collaborating with Laureate Fellow Baker and other Laureate Fellowship project collaborators. About the role Level A – ( $113k - $121k) plus 17% Superannuation and annual leave loading Fixed Term – 2 years Full-time (35 hours per week) Specific responsibilities for this role include: Contribute both independently and as a team member in collaborative research with a focus to enhance the quality of research outcomes of the Laureate Fellowship project. Design, configure, and run groundwater recharge model simulations at different scales to investigate the climate and environmental processes that lead to relationships between climate and environmental change and groundwater recharge. Conduct research in line with the project’s aims independently and under limited direction of the supervisor. Work collaboratively with other researchers among the Laureate fellowship team and the groundwater team at the TU Dresden. Maintain a strong focus on communicating research findings by publishing in highly ranked journals and presenting to peers at local, national and global conferences. Contribute independently or as a team member to the collegiate life of the Laureate Fellowship team such as assisting with the supervision of PhD students, committee memberships, participating in workshops, etc. Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Cooperate with all health and safety policies and procedures of the university and take all reasonable care to ensure that your actions or omissions do not impact on the health and safety of yourself or others. About the successful applicant: (Selection Criteria) PhD (or soon to be awarded) in groundwater hydrology or related areas such as hydrology, climate modelling, physics or mathematics. Demonstrated ability to carry out scientific research independently and as part of a collaborative team. Strong research and publication track record (relative to opportunity) in an area listed in the position summary. Evidence of high level analytical and problem-solving skills. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Evidence of experience in using groundwater models and processing large datasets Demonstrated programming skills (e.g. MATLAB, Python, R). An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health and safety responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. You should systematically address the selection criteria listed within the position description in your application. Please apply online - applications will not be accepted if sent to the contact listed. Contact: Andy Baker E: a.baker@unsw.edu.au Applications close: October 29 th , 2026 Find out more about working at UNSW at www.unsw.edu.au UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543377/communications-officer-internal-corporate-communications-team": {
      "title": "Communications Officer (Internal- Corporate Communications Team)",
      "description": "Communications Officer (Internal- Corporate Communications Team) Apply now Job no: 543377 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Marketing / Communications Employment Type: Full time, continuing Remuneration: $118k - $128k (depending on experience) + 17% super + leave loading Location: Kensington, NSW (Hybrid Working) For more than 75 years, UNSW has driven progress that benefits all, through learning, teaching and research. We are determined to leave our world better than we found it. Big minds solving big problems. And we’re only just getting started. This role sits within the Division of Societal Impact, Equity and Engagement (SIEE) which is responsible for supporting a diverse and inclusive staff and student community and working with partner communities and broader society towards positive change. Why your role matters As Communications Officer (Internal), you will support the delivery of a trusted, high‑impact corporate communications service, developing and implementing communications plans that support clear and consistent University‑wide messaging. Sitting within the Corporate Communications team, you will build relationships with your key stakeholders to promote the achievements and activities of the University and its staff through  internal communications channels , ensuring staff are informed, connected and engaged. The Communications Officer (Internal) will report to the Communications Manager (Internal) and has no direct reports. Your day to day includes: Write and disseminate UNSW stories and messages coordinating and delivering University-wide communications, including Inside UNSW and all-staff emails. Create clear, engaging and consistent content that keeps staff informed, connected and engaged with what’s happening across the University. Partner with stakeholders across UNSW, providing internal communications advice, identifying effective channels and approaches. Develop a range of internal communications plans and content, translating complex information into accessible messages for diverse audiences. Create and maintain internal communications resources, tools and user the support effective and consistent communications. Use insights, feedback and data to understand what’s working and continually improve the reach and impact of UNSW’s internal communications channels. For a full overview of the role accountabilities, please refer to the position description found under the job posting at Jobs@UNSW Who you are: We’re looking for a great communicator with experience in delivering internal communications across a range of platforms, with the following skills and experience: Tertiary qualifications with a depth of relevant experience in communications or marketing, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience in developing, delivering and measuring internal communications within a complex organisational environment. Understanding of target audience, with a highly effective writing style in high-impact messaging to internal audiences. Demonstrated experience in distilling complex information into simple messages for communication to a diverse stakeholder. Proven ability to liaise effectively with internal stakeholders to achieve desired outcomes. How to Apply: Submit your CV and a one-page cover letter detailing your interest and suitability for the job (as per the skills and experience in the job description) before Thursday, 15 October 2026, by 11:55pm. Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise the contributions of our staff make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours) Additional three days leave over University summer shutdown period Career development opportunities Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness: Fitness Passport). Job queries contact: Katherine Molloy, Talent Acquisition Consultant E: katherine.molloy@unsw.edu.au (applications to the address cannot be accepted, please apply online) #LI-KM1 UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. Position Description Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543400/senior-project-officer-university-strategy-office": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer - University Strategy Office",
      "description": "Senior Project Officer - University Strategy Office Apply now Job no: 543400 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Project Management Employment Type: Full Time, Continuing Remuneration: Level 7, $118,483 - $128,233 + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Kensington Campus, NSW (hybrid) Why Your Role Matters: The Strategy Office facilitates the implementation of the UNSW Strategy: Progress for All through effective planning, delivery and reporting aligned to the strategic vision. Working within the Office of the Provost, the team leads the development and execution of the strategic plan including the communications and engagement across the university, providing strategic foresights and research to inform strategic planning. The Strategy Office supports faculties and divisions in aligning with UNSW priorities, designs and drives flagship strategic initiatives, and monitors performance and progress toward achieving strategic objectives. The Senior Project Officer plays an important role in project management of initiatives and events, communication and engagement with faculties and divisions to support the development and implementation of strategic programs and projects of the UNSW Strategy, aimed at raising the University's global profile and impact. The role of Senior Project Officer reports to the Manager, Strategic Alliances and Initiatives and has no direct reports. Key Responsibilities: Work with faculty and divisional leaders to design and establish initiatives under the UNSW Strategy: Progress for All. Maintain strong working relationships with faculties and divisions to implement initiatives under the Impact Pathways and Impact Focus Areas of the Strategy. Work with functional units to obtain updates and prepare reports on Strategy progress for University Leadership Team (ULT) and others to ensure project management. Monitor and report on outcomes of strategic initiatives to the Vice Chancellor and Provost, and UNSW Council. Develop, implement and manage specific projects, initiatives or events for the UNSW Strategy Office in conjunction with the agreed business plans. Oversee the development of project plans and schedules and participate in planning processes as required. Support university governance and management structures, liaising with other administrative units across UNSW and partner institutions to ensure projects are managed and delivered in a timely and effective manner, and aligned appropriately to UNSW processes. Establish and maintain systems to manage projects and record information. Contribute to and support the development of proposals, business cases, guidelines and other business documents. Provide high level administrative support in the pursuit of project implementation, including minute taking, travel and budget management, website maintenance. Monitor and escalate, as necessary, complex and potentially sensitive issues and concerns that might affect the progress of UNSW strategic initiatives. Skills and Experience: Tertiary degree or equivalent in a relevant discipline with subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent level of knowledge gained through any other combination of relevant education, training and/or experience. Demonstrated experience in project management, including setting up systems for stakeholder engagement and communication, tracking and project reporting. High level organisational skills and demonstrated ability to prioritise workloads to ensure timely delivery of tasks. Advanced problem-solving and analytical skills. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build and sustain effective professional relationships with stakeholders. Excellent written and verbal communication skills with strong attention to detail and ability to prioritise and control workload with a positive sense of initiative. Ability to work independently or with minimal supervision, show initiative and contribute positively to a team. Proven ability to drive collaboration and successfully manage competing priorities. For further details on role responsibilities and skills & experience required, refer to the position description found under the position listing on Jobs@UNSW How to Apply: Submit your CV & Cover Letter by 11:55pm, Thursday 15th October 2026 Please note: Sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Contact: Rachel Darcy r.darcy@unsw.edu.au - Talent Acquisition Team Please apply directly through the website , applications sent to the contact above will not be accepted. UNSW Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do.We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Additional 3 days leave during December shutdown period. Career development opportunities Up to 50% discount on UNSW courses Flexible 17% Superannuation contributions, additional leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice. Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Pre-Employment Checks: As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543498/project-officer": {
      "title": "Project Officer",
      "description": "Project Officer Apply now Job no: 543498 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Project Management Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing Remuneration: Level 6: $108,021 - $115,233 + 17% super + leave loading Location: UNSW Paddington Campus About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community, a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Why Your Role Matters: The Project Officer plays a key role within the Faculty of Arts, Design & Architecture, providing practical and efficient project support for project teams and key stakeholders on the Paddington Campus. The role provides project coordination and support, reporting and analytical support, develops communications, builds key internal and external relationships, and provides administrative support for key programs and projects. The position reports to the School of Art & Design Senior School Manager, supports the School and relevant School Leadership, and has no direct reports. Responsibilities Summary: Support the delivery of Paddington Campus projects and initiatives to achieve operational and strategic goals, including the Annual Graduation Exhibition. Support the development of project plans and schedules and participate in planning processes as required. Monitor, track and report on project deliverables to ensure time, cost and quality metrics align with approved project plans. Provide practical and proactive project support and administrative services to project teams and stakeholders, including monitoring project risks and issues and providing solutions where applicable. Coordinate project administration, including workshops and meetings, minute taking, assigned actions and follow-ups, and project status reporting. Review, action and monitor administration requests from Paddington Academic Research units, including purchasing, financial administration, operational and space support, using Faculty shared administration units as appropriate. Develop and manage effective communications with key internal and external stakeholders for Paddington Campus projects. Support adherence to UNSW governance pathways and assist with the implementation and application of policies, guidelines and procedures for the effective operation of the Paddington Campus and School of Art & Design. For more information regarding the responsibilities for this role, please refer to the Position Description at JOBS@UNSW. Skills and Experience Summary: Relevant tertiary qualification with subsequent relevant experience, or equivalent competence gained through any combination of education, training and experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a high level of attention to detail. Sound stakeholder management skills, with the ability to liaise effectively with a range of stakeholders. Experience using a range of computer systems and applications, including Microsoft Office 365; knowledge of web and intranet applications such as SharePoint and Mailchimp is an advantage. Excellent time management skills, with demonstrated ability to respond to changing priorities, manage multiple tasks and meet competing deadlines using judgement and initiative. Project experience, with an understanding of the project life cycle and development of project plans, objectives and documentation. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and productively within a team, while also taking initiative and working independently when managing competing demands. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world.  Our benefits include: Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) How to Apply: Submit your CV and a cover letter outlining your suitability for the role in relation to the selection criteria (listed under ‘skills & experience’ on the PD) via the application portal before 11:30pm on October 15. A copy of the Position Description can be found on JOBS@UNSW. Get in Touch: For queries regarding the recruitment process, contact Lucy Gerondis, Talent Acquisition Partner, UNSW: l.gerondis@unsw.edu.au For queries regarding the role, contact Steven Shears, Senior School Manager: s.shears@unsw.edu.au Applications sent via email will not be accepted, please apply via the application portal. Pre-Employment Checks : As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543532/paralegal": {
      "title": "Paralegal",
      "description": "Paralegal Apply now Job no: 543532 Work type: Casual Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Administration, Legal At UNSW, we pride ourselves on being a workplace where the best people come to do their best work. Ranked among the top 50 universities globally, we attract the highest potential students from Australia and around the world. Our researchers tackle the biggest economic, business, and social challenges with partners around the world. Position Summary Level 4 The Paralegal is responsible for the provision of sound paralegal and administrative support to members of the Legal & Compliance team. This role will: Undertake paralegal and administrative work, such as conducting legal research, reviewing, analysing and summarising documents, and drafting correspondence and legal documents. Follow clear instructions from members of the UNSW Legal & Compliance team. Ensure that matter documentation and records are of an appropriate quality and maintained in accordance with Legal & Compliance’s record keeping requirements. This includes managing any conflicts of interest and the proper handling of confidential or sensitive information consistent with the obligations of the legal profession. Cooperate with all policies and procedures of the University and take all reasonable care to ensure your actions or omissions do not impact on the psychosocial or physical health and safety of yourself and others. A person engaged is generally expected to have the following skills and experience: Close to completion or has completed a Law degree; Highly developed written and verbal communication skills and the proven ability to liaise with a range of stakeholders; High level of attention to detail; Proficient level of computer literacy, with sound working knowledge/skills in Microsoft Office applications; and. Ability to complete legal tasks independently and to raise questions or issues as necessary. Additional pre-employment checks required for this position •            Criminal Records Check •            Academic Transcript Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks: National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. If you require any further information regarding the role, please contact Michael Stevens via michael.steven@unsw.edu.au. To submit an application for the role, please do so via the 'Apply Now' button. Please note, no applications will be accepted via email. #LI-DNI Position Description Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/539070/senior-business-analyst-student-systems-team": {
      "title": "Senior Business Analyst – Student Systems team",
      "description": "Senior Business Analyst – Student Systems team Apply now Job no: 539070 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology, Student Services, Analyst Employment Type: Full Time Permanent Remuneration: $127351 - $140,838 + 17% super (level 8) Location: Kensington UNSW Campus (hybrid) Why Your Role Matters The Senior Business Analyst helps deliver student systems transformation and enhancement initiatives across UNSW. Working in Student Systems, you will improve the systems, processes and services that support the student lifecycle. You will analyse end-to-end processes, engage stakeholders, identify opportunities to simplify ways of working, and translate business needs into clear requirements. Across the project lifecycle, you will work with project managers, developers, testers, business owners and student administration stakeholders to turn strategic goals into practical, user-focused solutions. Experience in student administration or higher education systems is desirable, but strong business analysis capability is essential. Reporting to the Manager, Student Systems (Projects), you may supervise one direct report. Key responsibilities include: Lead business analysis across student systems projects and enhancements Understand current-state processes, pain points, business needs and future-state requirements with stakeholders Elicit and document requirements, process maps, user stories, functional requirements and acceptance criteria Identify opportunities to simplify and improve processes across the student lifecycle Bridge business and technical teams, translating needs into practical solutions Contribute to options analysis, impact assessments, business cases and decision materials Support user acceptance testing, training, business readiness and go-live activities Advise on processes, system capabilities, risks, dependencies and fit-for-purpose solutions Support continuous improvement and mentor junior business analysts as required About You You are a Business Analyst who can understand how work gets done, identify what can be improved, and turn business needs into clear, practical requirements. This is a business analysis role, not a functional analyst role. You will work with business areas to understand complex processes, simplify ways of working and deliver improvements that enhance the student and staff experience.  You are a collaborative communicator who builds trust with stakeholders and brings structure, clarity and sound judgement to complex or ambiguous problems. You will bring: Demonstrated Business Analyst experience analysing complex end-to-end processes, gathering requirements and identifying improvements Experience gathering, documenting and validating requirements with senior stakeholders Ability to translate business needs into requirements, process documentation, user stories, acceptance criteria and delivery artefacts Experience supporting system, process or service improvements in project environments Excellent stakeholder engagement skills, including workshop facilitation and building shared understanding Strong analytical, problem-solving and attention-to-detail skills Ability to manage competing priorities in a complex organisation Exposure to Waterfall, Agile or hybrid delivery approaches Experience contributing to transformation, process improvement or operational change initiatives Higher education, student administration or student lifecycle experience is highly regarded. Knowledge of TechnologyOne, Callista, SITS, Ellucian Banner or similar platforms is advantageous; however, the role focuses on business analysis rather than technical configuration. This is a full-time, continuing role based at Kensington with hybrid flexibility, Monday to Friday. Candidates must have valid working rights in Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available. Please submit your resume and a cover letter explaining your interest in the role and addressing the “About You” section. You will also answer additional questions as part of the application and can save your progress before returning. Applications close: 13th October 2026 11.55PM Get in touch (job-related queries only): Anne de Voronine, a.de_voronine@unsw.edu.au Applications will not be accepted if sent directly to this contact. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/538390/program-manager": {
      "title": "Program Manager",
      "description": "Program Manager Apply now Job no: 538390 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Information Technology Full time role, 24 month fixed term, within UNSW IT as a Program Manager - IT Kensington, Sydney location, 2-3 days in the office, Hybrid working About UNSW: UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. Yes, we’re a large organisation with a diverse and talented community; a community doing extraordinary things. Together, we are driven to be thoughtful, practical, and purposeful in all we do. Taking this combined approach is what makes our work matter. It’s the reason we’re one of the top 20 universities in the world (QS top 20) and a member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you want a career where you can thrive, be challenged and do meaningful work, you’re in the right place. The Program Manager will be responsible for delivering modernized IT infrastructure aligned with UNSW’s Technology Strategy and engineering standards. The role ensures strategic alignment, enables capability, transforms operations, and realizes program benefits. This role will draw on their extensive industry experience and technical knowledge to contribute to communications infrastructure design, ensuring cost effective delivery of high-quality IT infrastructure to time and to budget for all major capital works and large-scale refurbishment initiatives. This role works under broad direction, is self-initiated, and performs an extensive range and variety of complex work activities. The Program Manager will utilise strong stakeholder engagement to work in consultation with internal teams within UNSW IT and external customers to specify and manage infrastructure projects involving network, critical infrastructure and the deployment of desktop, print and AV services. The Program Manager will maintain collaboration with the IT PPMO to ensure adherence to the defined project delivery framework and is aligned to project processes and lifecycle governance. Specific accountabilities for this role include: Provide strategic leadership and advice around the delivery of complex programs to achieve the business objectives, business benefits & business value. This includes the ongoing management of scope, budget risks and issues, control, monitor and report end to end program status. Lead various IT project teams to deliver multiple projects in a cost-effective manner with high customer focus. Act as the single point of contact and point of escalation for all our stakeholders and other business units. Act as the senior technology authority within the program, ensuring all design and delivery decisions align with UNSW Technology’s strategic vision and intended outcomes. Own workstream planning, scheduling, scope, risks and issues, dependency mapping, and delivery performance, while aligning to IT infrastructure office standards, division specific frameworks, and reporting requirements. Drive a strong delivery culture built on accountability, adaptability, and collaboration. Hold accountability for the development and management of large, complex project budgets, including financial forecasting, variance analysis, and ensuring ongoing alignment with approved funding. Manage project resources within budget and maintain financial forecasting for IT PPMO and UPO monthly reporting purposes. Actively engage, influence, and collaborate with academic and professional leaders. Ensure their perspectives are reflected in plans, risks, and treatments, and build consensus on delivery approaches, sequencing, and issue resolution. Direct and inspire multidisciplinary teams across UNSW and vendor partners. Model purpose-driven leadership and create an environment where delivery excellence, resilience, and trust are standard. Set and manage the performance of IT systems integrators and technology vendors. Hold partners accountable for scope, deliverables, and quality, ensuring alignment with UNSW’s values, commercial arrangements, and program governance and outcomes. Negotiate the coordination of resources from areas within UNSWIT and business areas to engage in the development and delivery of the IT infrastructure Define IT specific program benefits and outcomes; establish baseline measures and track progress to ensure that business value is achieved and sustained. Lead the development and delivery of a program of work with large infrastructure projects on time and within budget. Manage dependencies with UNSW colleagues to ensure Technology outcomes are seamlessly embedded into the UNSW ecosystem. Bring deep IT workforce transformation expertise to anticipate risks, navigate uncertainty, and influence program direction with foresight and pragmatism. Provide mentorship to project members, offering strategic coaching, professional development guidance, and day-to-day leadership support Align with and actively demonstrate the Code of Conduct and Values. Ensure hazards and risks psychosocial and physical are identified and controlled for tasks, projects, and activities that pose a health and safety risk within your area of responsibility. Who you are: Extensive, demonstrable and recent experience leading the end-to-end delivery of large-scale IT project management with senior accountability for outcomes in highly federated, complex, and multi-stakeholder environments. Experience in senior transformation leadership roles with direct C-suite reporting and proven success delivering high-value, enterprise-wide solutions in large organisations. Tertiary qualifications in a relevant discipline, combined with substantial professional experience, or equivalent capability gained through a mix of education, executive training, and hands-on IT program leadership. Extensive experience and knowledge of project management methodologies and technique Deep expertise business process transformation, operating model design, and lifecycle management, with a track record of delivering key IT Management capabilities; higher education or similarly federated sector experience is highly desirable. Demonstrated capability to establish, lead, and inspire high-performing, cross-functional teams spanning business, technology and partner domains. High level organisational skills with a demonstrated ability to manage the competing demands of multiple, simultaneous projects. Sound financial literacy skills, including the ability to forecast and manage budgets. Strong analytical skills, effective business planning and budgeting skills with demonstrated experience in strategic and operational planning. Strong stakeholder engagement and influencing skills, with the ability to engage credibly with senior executives, governance committees, and diverse communities of interest. A proven ability to navigate complexity, manage dependencies, trade-offs, and risks across multiple streams under conditions of ambiguity and scrutiny. Significant experience managing major vendors and delivery partners, including systems integrators, to ensure performance accountability, scope discipline, and alignment with organisational outcomes. A comprehensive understanding of transformation delivery disciplines, including program governance, benefits realisation, user-centred design, and organisational change management, with the ability to apply these to achieve measurable adoption and value. Exceptional communication skills with a record of producing executive-level briefings, business cases, reports, and presentations that distil complex information into actionable insights. Advanced industry certifications including Prince2 and/or PMBOK or equivalent, is desirable. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. Benefits and Culture Flexible Working Options (work from home, flexible hours etc) Career development opportunities 17% Superannuation contributions and additional leave loading payments Additional 3 days of leave over Christmas period Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness) For further details on the benefits, please visit https://www.jobs.unsw.edu.au/lifestyle-benefits How to Apply: please apply through the portal, we would like you to submit a full application including resume and addressing the who you are section. Applications close:  13th of October 2026 @ 11.30pm Pre-Employment Checks Aligned with UNSW’s focus on cultivating a workplace defined by safety, ethical conduct, and strong integrity preferred candidates will be required to participate in a combination of pre-employment checks relevant to the role they have applied for. These pre-employment checks may include a combination of some of the following checks:- National and International Criminal history checks Entitlement to work and ID checks Working With Children Checks Completion of a Gender-Based Violence Prevention Declaration Verification of relevant qualifications Verification of relevant professional membership Employment history and reference checks Financial responsibility assessments/checks. Medical Checks and Assessments Compliance with the necessary combination of these checks is a condition of employment at UNSW. Get in Touch: Jen MacLachlan j.maclachlan@unsw.edu.au Talent Acquisition Partner – UNSW IT Please apply through the application portal and not via the contact above. UNSW is committed to equity diversity and inclusion. Applications from women, people of culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, those living with disabilities, members of the LGBTIQ+ community; and people of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander descent, are encouraged. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Position Description Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://external-careers.jobs.unsw.edu.au/en/job/543196/statistical-consultant-research": {
      "title": "Statistical Consultant (Research)",
      "description": "Statistical Consultant (Research) Apply now Job no: 543196 Work type: Full Time Location: Sydney, NSW Categories: Post Doctoral Research Associate, Associate Lecturer, Other A great next step after your PhD – apply your statistical expertise to a wide variety of real-world research problems and build your project experience. Varied and interesting work – contribute to diverse research projects across UNSW, working with researchers from different disciplines and tackling a broad range of applied statistical challenges. Collaborative team + publication opportunities – join a friendly, supportive team while contributing to research that can lead to publications. Why Your Role Matters $95,036 -$126,711 + 17% super (*minimum $118,467 with PhD) 12-month fixed term contract at UNSW Kensington Full-time (35 hours week) Stats Central is a team of nine statisticians and two bioinformaticians offering UNSW researchers support and training in study design and analysis, as part of the university’s 2025 Strategy. It is based in the Mark Wainwright Analytical Centre in the Division of Research and Enterprise, a centralised unit that manages major research infrastructure at UNSW and provides relevant technical and consulting expertise. The Statistical Consultant (Research) will provide advice on or lead study design, analysis, and communication of results in a cross-disciplinary context on research contracts with partners around the university. About UNSW UNSW isn’t like other places you’ve worked. We are a large organisation with a diverse and highly talented community doing extraordinary work. We are driven to be thoughtful, practical and purposeful in all we do, and this collective approach is what gives our work impact. It is also why UNSW is consistently ranked among the world’s top 20 universities in the QS World University Rankings and is a proud member of Australia’s prestigious Group of Eight. If you’re seeking a career where you can thrive, be challenged and contribute to meaningful work, you’re in the right place. Benefits and Culture: People are at the core of everything we do. We recognise it is the contributions of our staff who make UNSW one of the best universities in Australia and the world. Our benefits include: Flexible working arrangements and are committed to supporting work-life balance while delivering meaningful outcomes. 17% superannuation contributions, annual leave-loading payments and salary sacrifice option Discounts and entitlements (retail, education, fitness passport) Affordable on-campus parking Who You Are: You have a PhD in Applied Statistics or related discipline, and can demonstrate the following skills and experience: Design and analysis of clinical trials, and advanced statistical techniques common in health and behavioural research, such as generalised linear mixed models, survival analysis, time series, data imputation methods and meta-analysis. Ability to meet project requirements and deadlines and at a high level with minimal supervision. Ability to work effectively as a member of a team, and the capacity for independent decision- making using initiative. Establish and manage consultative, productive relationships with researchers from different backgrounds and communicate statistical concepts clearly to non-statisticians. Provide effective statistical mentoring to research staff. Ability to develop statistical methodology or statistical software applications, and to write code in multiple statistical packages. An understanding of and commitment to UNSW’s aims, objectives and values in action, together with relevant policies and guidelines. Knowledge of health & safety (psychosocial and physical) responsibilities and commitment to attending relevant health and safety training. To Apply : If this opportunity interests you, please submit your CV and Cover Letter before 11.55pm on Monday 19th October 2026 via the APPLY button. A copy of the Position Description is available via Jobs@UNSW. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not available for this role, valid Australian working rights are required on application. Pre Employment Checks As part of our recruitment process candidates may be required to undergo pre-employment screening, which may include reference checks, qualification verification, right-to-work verification, and criminal history screening where relevant to the role. Contact: Shiree Thomas – Talent Acquisition Consultant e: shiree.thomas@unsw.edu.au Please note applications sent directly to the contact person will not be accepted UNSW is committed to evolving a culture that embraces equity and supports a diverse and inclusive community where everyone can participate fairly, in a safe and respectful environment. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds and encourage applications from people of diverse gender, sexual orientation, cultural and linguistic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander background, people with disability and those with caring and family responsibilities. UNSW provides workplace adjustments for people with disability, and access to flexible work options for eligible staff. The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. #LI-ST1 Position Description Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    }
  },
